[36m(TaskRunner pid=2351477)[0m ["To convert the point \\((0, 3)\\) from rectangular (Cartesian) coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the values of \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\).\n\nThe formulas to convert from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y)\\) to polar coordinates \\((r, \\theta)\\) are:\n1. \\(r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\)\n2. \\(\\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0 \n\\end{cases}\\)\n\nGiven the point \\((0, 3)\\), we can see that \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 3\\). Using the formulas above:\n\n1. \\(r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\\)\n2. Since \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y > 0\\), \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\)\n\nSo the polar coordinates are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate theta\nif x == 0 and y > 0:\n    theta = math.pi / 2\nelif x == 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = -math.pi / 2\nelif x > 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x)\nelif x < 0 and y >= 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) + math.pi\nelif x < 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) - math.pi\n\n# Ensure theta is in the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates for the point \\((0, 3)\\) are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})}\n\\]", "To convert the point $(0,3)$ from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to determine the values of $r$ and $\\theta$.\n\n1. **Calculate $r$:**\n   The formula for $r$ is given by:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\n   \\]\n   For the point $(0,3)$, we have $x = 0$ and $y = 3$. So,\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate $\\theta$:**\n   The formula for $\\theta$ is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n   \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n   \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\geq 0 \\\\\n   \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n   \\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n   -\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n   \\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   For the point $(0,3)$, we have $x = 0$ and $y = 3$. So, $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $(0,3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate theta\ntheta = math.atan2(y, x)\n\n# Ensure theta is in the range [0, 2*pi)\nif theta < 0:\n    theta += 2 * math.pi\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point $(0,3)$ are $\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}\n\\]", "To convert the point \\((0, 3)\\) from rectangular (Cartesian) coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the values of \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\). The formulas to convert from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y)\\) to polar coordinates \\((r, \\theta)\\) are:\n\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\text{undefined} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y = 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point \\((0, 3)\\), we can see that \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 3\\). Using the formulas above:\n\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3 \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo the polar coordinates of the point \\((0, 3)\\) are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Rectangular coordinates\nx = 0\ny = 3\n\n# Calculate r\nr = math.sqrt(x**2 + y**2)\n\n# Calculate theta\nif x == 0 and y > 0:\n    theta = math.pi / 2\nelif x == 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = -math.pi / 2\nelif x > 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x)\nelif x < 0 and y >= 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) + math.pi\nelif x < 0 and y < 0:\n    theta = math.atan(y / x) - math.pi\nelse:\n    theta = 0\n\nprint(((r, theta)))\n```\n```output\n(3.0, 1.5707963267948966)\n```\nThe polar coordinates of the point \\((0, 3)\\) are \\((3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(3, \\frac{\\pi}{2})}\n\\]", "To convert the point \\((0, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the values of \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\).\n\nThe formulas to convert from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y)\\) to polar coordinates \\((r, \\theta)\\) are:\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\begin{cases} \n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) & \\text{if } x > 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y \\ge 0 \\\\\n\\arctan\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi & \\text{if } x < 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y > 0 \\\\\n-\\frac{\\pi}{2} & \\text{if } x = 0 \\text{ and } y < 0 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the point \\((0, 3)\\):\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{0^2 + 3^2} 