"2 * 3 + (4 * 5 + 1)",\n    "(2 + 1) * 3 * 4 * 5"\n]\n\n# Evaluate all expressions and collect distinct results\ndistinct_values = set(evaluate_expression(expr) for expr in expressions)\n\n# Output the distinct values and their count\nprint((distinct_values, len(distinct_values)))\n```\n```output\n({144, 150, 71, 140, 27, 180, 121}, 7)\n```\nThe distinct values obtained from the expression $2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1$ by inserting parentheses are 121, 144, 150, 140, 71, 27, and 180. Thus, the number of distinct values is 7.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).', "To determine how many distinct values can be obtained by inserting parentheses into the expression \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1\\), we need to consider all possible ways to group the terms. The expression can be grouped in different ways, and we need to evaluate each of these groupings to find the distinct values.\n\nThe expression \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1\\) can be grouped in the following ways:\n\n1. \\(((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1\\)\n2. \\((2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 4)) \\cdot 5 + 1\\)\n3. \\(2 \\cdot ((3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5) + 1\\)\n4. \\(2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5)) + 1\\)\n5. \\((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5) + 1\\)\n6. \\(2 \\cdot ((3 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5) + 1\\) (This is the same as grouping 3, so we don't need to consider it again)\n7. \\(((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot 4) \\cdot 5 + 1\\) (This is the same as grouping 1, so we don't need to consider it again)\n8. \\((2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 4)) \\cdot 5 + 1\\) (This is the same as grouping 2, so we don't need to consider it again)\n9. \\(2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5)) + 1\\) (This is the same as grouping 4, so we don't need to consider it again)\n10. \\((2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot (4 \\cdot 5) + 1\\) (This is the same as grouping 5, so we don't need to consider it again)\n\nWe can see that there are essentially 5 unique ways to group the terms. Now, let's evaluate each of these groupings to find the distinct values.\n\n1. \\(((2 \\cdot 3