of each year.\n\nThe formula for the future value of a series of deposits (annuity) with compound interest is given by:\n\n\\[ FV = P \\left( \\frac{(1 + r)^n - 1}{r} \\right) \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( FV \\) is the future value of the deposits.\n- \\( P \\) is the amount of each deposit.\n- \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (in decimal form).\n- \\( n \\) is the number of years.\n\nIn this problem:\n- \\( FV = 66,200 \\)\n- \\( P = 20,000 \\)\n- \\( n = 3 \\)\n\nWe need to find the minimal \\( r \\) that satisfies this equation. Let's use Python to solve for \\( r \\).\n\nFirst, we will set up the equation and use a numerical method to find the root. We'll use the `scipy.optimize` library to find the root of the equation \\( FV - P \\left( \\frac{(1 + r)^n - 1}{r} \\right) = 0 \\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python.\n```python\nimport scipy.optimize as opt\n\n# Define the function representing the equation\ndef equation(r):\n    P = 20000\n    n = 3\n    FV = 66200\n    return FV - P * ((1 + r)**n - 1) / r\n\n# Use scipy.optimize to find the root of the equation\n# We need to provide an initial guess for r. Let's start with 0.05 (5%).\ninitial_guess = 0.05\nsolution = opt.root(equation, initial_guess)\n\n# Extract the solution and convert it to a percentage\nminimal_interest_rate = solution.x[0] * 100\nprint(minimal_interest_rate)\n```\n```output\n10.000000000000009\n```\nThe minimal compound interest rate that the savings account must provide is \\( \\boxed{10} \\)%.", "To find the minimal compound interest rate that the savings account must provide, we need to use the formula for the future value of a series of deposits (annuities). The formula for the future value of an ordinary annuity (where deposits are made at the end of each period) is:\n\n\\[ FV = P \\left( \\frac{(1 + r)^n - 1}{r} \\right) \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( FV \\) is the future value of the annuity.\n- \\( P \\) is the amount of each deposit.\n- \\( r \\) is the interest rate per period.\n- \\( n \\) is the number of periods.\n\nIn this problem:\n- \\( FV = 66,200 \\)\n- \\( P = 20,000 \\)\n- \\( n = 3 \\)\n\nWe need to find the interest rate \\( r \\) that satisfies this equation. Let's set up the equation and solve for \\( r \\):\n\n\\[ 66,200 = 20,000 \\left( \\frac{(1 + r)^3 - 1}{r} \\right) \\]\n\nFirst, we can simplify the equation by dividing both sides by 20,000:\n\n\\[ 3.31 = \\frac{(1 + r)^3 - 1}{r} \\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[ 3.31r = (1 + r)^3 - 1 \\]\n\nor\n\n\\[ (1 + r)^3 = 3.31r + 1 \\]\n\nWe can solve this equation using numerical methods. Let's use Python to find the value of \\( r \\).\n```python\nfrom scipy.optimize import fsolve\n\n# Define the equation to solve\ndef equation(r):\n    return (1 + r)**3 - (3.31 * r + 1)\n\n# Initial guess for r\ninitial_guess = 0.05\n\n# Solve for r\nr_solution = fsolve(equation, initial_guess)[0]\n\n# Convert r to percentage\nr_percentage = r_solution * 100\nprint(r_percentage)\n```\n```output\n10.000000000000002\n```\nThe minimal compound interest rate that the savings account must provide is \\( \\boxed{10} \\) percent.", "To determine the minimal compound interest rate that the savings account must provide, we need to use the formula for the future value of an ordinary annuity. The formula for the future value of an ordinary annuity is given by:\n\n\\[ FV = P \\left( \\frac{(1 + r)^n - 1}{r} \\right) \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( FV \\) is the future value of the annuity.\n- \\( P \\) is the payment amount per period.\n- \\( r \\) is the interest rate per period.\n- \\( n \\) is the number of periods.\n\nIn this problem:\n- \\( FV = 66200 \\)\n- \\( P = 20000 \\)\n- \\( n = 3 \\)\n\nWe need to find the interest rate \\( r \\). Rearranging the formula to solve for \\( r \\):\n\n\\[ 66200 = 20000 \\left( \\frac{(1 + r)^3 - 1}{r} \\right) \\]\n\n\\[ 3.31 = \\frac{(1 + r)^3 - 1}{r} \\]\n\nThis equation is not straightforward to solve algebraically, so we will use numerical methods to find the value of \\( r \\