 integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\( \\boxed{6} \\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity. The \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity are the solutions to the equation \\( z^n = 1 \\).\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\). We can use the substitution \\( w = z^2 \\), which transforms the equation into a quadratic equation in \\( w \\):\n\\[ w^2 + w + 1 = 0. \\]\nThe roots of this quadratic equation are:\n\\[ w = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}. \\]\nSo, the roots of the original polynomial are the square roots of these values:\n\\[ z = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}}, \\quad z = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}}. \\]\nThese roots can be expressed in polar form. Notice that the complex numbers \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) are the primitive 6th roots of unity, since they are the complex solutions to \\( w^2 + w + 1 = 0 \\), and they are the square roots of the 6th roots of unity. Therefore, the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are the 12th roots of unity, because they are the square roots of the 6th roots of unity.\n\nTo verify, we can check that the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are indeed the 12th roots of unity. The 12th roots of unity are the solutions to \\( z^{12} = 1 \\). We can check that the roots of the polynomial satisfy this equation.\n\nLet's use Python to verify this. We will find the roots of the polynomial and check if they are 12th roots of unity.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the polynomial coefficients\ncoefficients = [1, 0, 1, 0, 1]\n\n# Find the roots of the polynomial\nroots = np.roots(coefficients)\n\n# Check if the roots are 12th roots of unity\nn = 12\