centage_decrease)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe percentage decrease in the area of the square when it is transformed into a rectangle by decreasing the lengths of two opposite sides by 40% and increasing the lengths of the other two sides by 50% is \\(\\boxed{10\\%}\\).", "Let's solve the problem step-by-step.\n\n1. **Initial Area of the Square:**\n   Let the side length of the original square be \\( s \\). The area of the square is \\( s^2 \\).\n\n2. **New Dimensions of the Rectangle:**\n   After the changes, the lengths of two opposite sides are decreased by 40%, so the new length of these sides is \\( s \\times (1 - 0.40) = 0.60s \\). The lengths of the other two sides are increased by 50%, so the new length of these sides is \\( s \\times (1 + 0.50) = 1.50s \\).\n\n3. **New Area of the Rectangle:**\n   The area of the new rectangle is \\( 0.60s \\times 1.50s = 0.90s^2 \\).\n\n4. **Percentage Decrease in Area:**\n   The decrease in area is \\( s^2 - 0.90s^2 = 0.10s^2 \\). The percentage decrease in area is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{0.10s^2}{s^2} \\times 100 = 10\\%\n   \\]\n\nNow let's verify this with Python code.\n```python\n# Initial side length of the square (we can use any value since it will cancel out)\ns = 10\n\n# New dimensions of the rectangle\nnew_length = s * (1 - 0.40)\nnew_width = s * (1 + 0.50)\n\n# Initial area of the square\ninitial_area = s ** 2\n\n# New area of the rectangle\nnew_area = new_length * new_width\n\n# Percentage decrease in area\npercentage_decrease = ((initial_area - new_area) / initial_area) * 100\nprint(percentage_decrease)\n```\n```output\n10.0\n```\nThe percentage decrease in the area of the square is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).", "Let's start by defining the original side length of the square as \\( s \\). The area of the original square is \\( s^2 \\).\n\nAfter the changes, the lengths of two opposite sides of the square are decreased by 40%, so the new length of these sides is \\( 0.6s \\). The lengths of the other two sides are increased by 50%, so the new length of these sides is \\( 1.5s \\).\n\nThe area of the new rectangle is \\( 0.6s \\times 1.5s = 0.9s^2 \\).\n\nThe decrease in area is \\( s^2 - 0.9s^2 = 0.1s^2 \\). The percent decrease in area is \\(\\frac{0.1s^2}{s^2} \\times 100 = 10\\%\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\n# Original side length of the square\r\ns = 1  # Since we are dealing with percentages, the actual value of s doesn't matter, we can assume s = 1\r\n\r\n# New dimensions of the rectangle\r\nnew_length = 0.6 * s\r\nnew_width = 1.5 * s\r\n\r\n# Original area of the square\r\noriginal_area = s * s\r\n\r\n# New area of the rectangle\r\nnew_area = new_length * new_width\r\n\r\n# Decrease in area\r\narea_decrease = original_area - new_area\r\n\r\n# Percent decrease in area\r\npercent_decrease = (area_decrease / original_area) * 100\r\nprint(percent_decrease)\n```\n```output\n10.000000000000009\n```\nThe percent decrease in the area of the square is \\(\\boxed{10\\%}\\).", 'Let the side length of the square be $x$. Then, the area of the square is $x^2$.\n\nThe lengths of two opposite sides of the square are decreased by $40\\%$, so the new length is $x - 0.4x = 0.6x$. The lengths of the other two sides are increased by $50\\%$, so the new length is $x + 0.5x = 1.5x$.\n\nThe area of the rectangle is $0.6x \\cdot 1.5x = 0.9x^2$.\n\nThe decrease in area is $x^2 - 0.9x^2 = 0.1x^2$.\n\nThe percent decrease in area is $\\frac{0.1x^2}{x^2} \\cdot 100\\% = \\boxed{10\\%}$.', "Let's start by defining the side length of the original square as \\( s \\). The area of the original square is \\( s^2 \\).\n\nAfter the transformation, the lengths of two opposite sides of the square are decreased by 40%, so the new length of these sides is \\( s \\times (1 - 0.40) = 0.60s \\). The lengths of the other two sides are increased by 50%, so the new length of these sides is \\( s \\times (1 + 0.50) = 1.50s \\).\n\nThe area of the new rectangle is \\( 0.60s \\times 1.50s = 0.90s^2 