ion becomes:\n   \\[\n   E = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c} = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} + 1 = \\frac{5}{2}\n   \\]\n   So, \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\) is a possible value for \\(E\\).\n\n4. **Checking the Range:**\n   To check if \\(E\\) can take any value greater than or equal to \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\), we can use the fact that the expression is continuous and can be made arbitrarily large by choosing \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) appropriately.\n\nLet\'s use Python and sympy to verify this.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, c = sp.symbols(\'a b c\', positive=True)\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = c/a + a/(b + c) + b/c\n\n# Check the value when a = b = c\nvalue_when_equal = expression.subs({a: 1, b: 1, c: 1})\n\n# Check the lower bound using AM-GM inequality\nlower_bound = 3 * sp.root(b / (b + c), 3)\n\n# Print the results\nprint(f"Value when a = b = c: {value_when_equal}")\nprint(f"Lower bound using AM-GM: {lower_bound}")\n```\n```output\nValue when a = b = c: 5/2\nLower bound using AM-GM: 3*(b/(b + c))**(1/3)\n```\nThe output confirms that when \\(a = b = c\\), the expression evaluates to \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\). The lower bound using the AM-GM inequality is \\(3 \\left(\\frac{b}{b + c}\\right)^{1/3}\\), which is less than 3 since \\(\\frac{b}{b + c} < 1\\).\n\nTo find the set of all possible values of the expression \\(\\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}\\), we need to show that the expression can take any value greater than or equal to \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\).\n\n1. **Lower Bound Analysis:**\n   We have already shown that when \\(a = b = c\\), the expression equals \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\). To show that the expression can take any value greater than \\(\\frac{5}{2}\\), we can consider the behavior of the expression as \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) vary.\n\n2. **Behavior of the Expression:**\n   If we fix \\(b\\) and vary \\(a\\) and \\(c\\), we can make the expression arbitrarily large. For example, if we let \\(a\\) be very small compared to \\(b\\) a