ute x = -2 into the polynomial\nremainder = P.subs(x, -2)\n\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n105\n```\nThe remainder when the polynomial \\( (5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1 \\) is divided by \\( x + 2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{105}\\).", "To find the remainder when the polynomial \\(P(x) = (5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\), we can use the Remainder Theorem. The Remainder Theorem states that the remainder of the division of a polynomial \\(P(x)\\) by a linear divisor \\(x - c\\) is \\(P(c)\\). In this case, the divisor is \\(x + 2\\), so we need to find \\(P(-2)\\).\n\nLet's substitute \\(x = -2\\) into the polynomial \\(P(x)\\):\n\n\\[P(-2) = (5(-2) + 9)^{611} + ((-2) + 5)^{11} + ((-2) - 1)^{11} + 3(-2)^2 + 1\\]\n\nSimplifying each term:\n\n1. \\(5(-2) + 9 = -10 + 9 = -1\\), so \\((-1)^{611} = -1\\).\n2. \\((-2) + 5 = 3\\), so \\(3^{11}\\).\n3. \\((-2) - 1 = -3\\), so \\((-3)^{11} = -3^{11}\\).\n4. \\(3(-2)^2 = 3(4) = 12\\).\n5. The constant term is \\(1\\).\n\nCombining these results, we get:\n\n\\[P(-2) = -1 + 3^{11} - 3^{11} + 12 + 1 = -1 + 12 + 1 = 12\\]\n\nSo the remainder when \\(P(x)\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\) is \\(12\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the polynomial function P(x)\ndef P(x):\n    return (5*x + 9)**611 + (x + 5)**11 + (x - 1)**11 + 3*x**2 + 1\n\n# Calculate P(-2)\nremainder = P(-2)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n12\n```\nThe remainder when the polynomial \\((5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the Remainder Theorem. The Remainder Theorem states that the remainder of the division of a polynomial \\(f(x)\\) by a linear divisor \\(x - c\\) is \\(f(c)\\). In this case, we are dividing by \\(x + 2\\), so we need to find the value of the polynomial at \\(x = -2\\).\n\nLet's denote the polynomial as \\(P(x) = (5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1\\). We need to find \\(P(-2)\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(x = -2\\) into the polynomial, we get:\n\\[P(-2) = (5(-2) + 9)^{611} + (-2 + 5)^{11} + (-2 - 1)^{11} + 3(-2)^2 + 1.\\]\n\nLet's break this down step by step:\n1. Calculate \\(5(-2) + 9 = -10 + 9 = -1\\), so \\((-1)^{611} = -1\\).\n2. Calculate \\(-2 + 5 = 3\\), so \\(3^{11}\\).\n3. Calculate \\(-2 - 1 = -3\\), so \\((-3)^{11} = -3^{11}\\).\n4. Calculate \\(3(-2)^2 = 3(4) = 12\\).\n5. Add the constant term \\(1\\).\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[P(-2) = (-1)^{611} + 3^{11} + (-3)^{11} + 12 + 1 = -1 + 3^{11} - 3^{11} + 12 + 1 = -1 + 12 + 1 = 12.\\]\n\nThe remainder when \\(P(x)\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\) is \\(12\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Define the polynomial function P(x)\ndef P(x):\n    return (5 * x + 9)**611 + (x + 5)**11 + (x - 1)**11 + 3 * x**2 + 1\n\n# Calculate P(-2)\nremainder = P(-2)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n12\n```\nThe remainder when \\((5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).", "To find the remainder when a polynomial \\(P(x)\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\), we can use the Remainder Theorem. The Remainder Theorem states that the remainder of the division of a polynomial \\(P(x)\\) by \\(x - c\\) is \\(P(c)\\). In this case, we need to find the remainder when \\(P(x) = (5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\), so we need to evaluate \\(P(-2)\\).\n\nLet's evaluate \\(P(-2)\\):\n\n\\[P(-2) = (5(-2) + 9)^{611} + ((-2) + 5)^{11} + ((-2) - 1)^{11} + 3(-2)^2 + 1\\]\n\nSimplifying each term:\n\n1. \\(5(-2) + 9 = -10 + 9 = -1\\), so \\((5(-2) + 9)^{611} = (-1)^{611} = -1\\) because 611 is an odd number.\n2. \\((-2) + 5 = 3\\), so \\(((-2) + 5)^{11} = 3^{11}\\).\n3. \\((-2) - 1 = -3\\), so \\(((-2) - 1)^{11} = (-3)^{11} = -3^{11}\\) because 11 is an odd number.\n4. \\(3(-2)^2 = 3(4) = 12\\).\n5. The constant term is 1.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\\[P(-2) = -1 + 3^{11} - 3^{11} + 12 + 1 = -1 + 0 + 12 + 1 = 12\\]\n\nSo the remainder when \\(P(x)\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\) is \\(12\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the polynomial function P(x)\ndef P(x):\n    return (5*x + 9)**611 + (x + 5)**11 + (x - 1)**11 + 3*x**2 + 1\n\n# Evaluate P(-2)\nremainder = P(-2)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n12\n```\nThe remainder when \\((5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1\\) is divided by \\(x + 2\\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).", "To find the remainder when $(5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1$ is divided by $x + 2$, we can use the Remainder Theorem. The Remainder Theorem states that the remainder of the division of a polynomial $f(x)$ by a linear divisor $x - c$ is $f(c)$. In this case, we are dividing by $x + 2$, which is equivalent to $x - (-2)$, so we need to evaluate the polynomial at $x = -2$.\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Evaluate $(5x + 9)^{611}$ at $x = -2$:\n   \\[\n   (5(-2) + 9)^{611} = (-10 + 9)^{611} = (-1)^{611} = -1\n   \\]\n\n2. Evaluate $(x + 5)^{11}$ at $x = -2$:\n   \\[\n   (-2 + 5)^{11} = 3^{11}\n   \\]\n\n3. Evaluate $(x - 1)^{11}$ at $x = -2$:\n   \\[\n   (-2 - 1)^{11} = (-3)^{11} = -3^{11}\n   \\]\n\n4. Evaluate $3x^2$ at $x = -2$:\n   \\[\n   3(-2)^2 = 3 \\cdot 4 = 12\n   \\]\n\n5. Evaluate the constant term $1$ at $x = -2$:\n   \\[\n   1\n   \\]\n\nNow, sum up all these values:\n\\[\n-1 + 3^{11} - 3^{11} + 12 + 1 = -1 + 0 + 12 + 1 = 12\n\\]\n\nSo the remainder when $(5x + 9)^{611} + (x + 5)^{11} + (x - 1)^{11} + 3x^2 + 1$ is divided by $x + 2$ is $12$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the polynomial function\ndef polynomial(x):\n    return (5*x + 9)**611 + (x + 5)**11 + (x - 1)**11 + 3*x**2 + 1\n\n# Evaluate the polynomial at x = -2\nremainder = polynomial(-2)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n12\n```\nThe remainder when $(5x + 9)^{611} + 