n{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "The given parameterization of the line is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis can be rewritten as two separate equations:\n\\[\nx = 8 + 2t,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -1 + 3t.\n\\]\nTo eliminate the parameter $t$, we can solve the first equation for $t$:\n\\[\nt = \\frac{x - 8}{2}.\n\\]\nNow substitute this expression for $t$ into the second equation:\n\\[\ny = -1 + 3\\left(\\frac{x - 8}{2}\\right).\n\\]\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\ny = -1 + \\frac{3x - 24}{2} = -1 + \\frac{3x}{2} - 12 = \\frac{3x}{2} - 13.\n\\]\nThis is now in the form $y = mx + b$, where $m = \\frac{3}{2}$ and $b = -13$. So the ordered pair $(m, b)$ is $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -13\\right)$.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the symbols\nx, y, t = symbols('x y t')\n\n# Define the parameterized equations\nx_eq = Eq(x, 8 + 2*t)\ny_eq = Eq(y, -1 + 3*t)\n\n# Solve the x equation for t\nt_expr = solve(x_eq, t)[0]\n\n# Substitute t into the y equation\ny_expr = y_eq.subs(t, t_expr)\n\n# Simplify the y equation to get it in the form y = mx + b\ny_simplified = y_expr.simplify()\n\n# Extract m and b from the simplified equation\nm = y_simplified.rhs.coeff(x)\nb = y_simplified.rhs.coeff(x, 0)\n\nprint(((m, b)))\n```\n```output\n(3/2, -13)\n```\nThe ordered pair $(m, b)$ is \\(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -13\\right)\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -13\\right)}\n\\]", "The given parameterized line is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{