ombinations of meals\ntotal_combinations = choices_yann * choices_camille\nprint(total_combinations)\n```\n```output\n90\n```\nThe total number of different combinations of meals that Yann and Camille can order, given that they refuse to order the same dish, is \\(\\boxed{90}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of different combinations of meals that Yann and Camille can order, given that they refuse to order the same dish and it matters who orders what (i.e., the order of the dishes matters).\n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Yann has 10 choices for his meal.\n2. Once Yann has chosen his meal, Camille has 9 remaining choices (since Camille cannot order the same dish as Yann).\n\nSo, the total number of different combinations of meals is the product of these choices: \\(10 \\times 9 = 90\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Number of items on the menu\nmenu_items = 10\n\n# Number of different combinations of meals\ncombinations = menu_items * (menu_items - 1)\nprint(combinations)\n```\n```output\n90\n```\nThe total number of different combinations of meals that Yann and Camille can order, given that they refuse to order the same dish and it matters who orders what, is \\(\\boxed{90}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to calculate the number of different combinations of meals that Yann and Camille can order, given that they refuse to order the same dish. Here's a step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Yann can choose any one of the 10 dishes.\n2. Once Yann has chosen a dish, Camille can choose any one of the remaining 9 dishes.\n\nSo, the total number of different combinations is the product of these choices: \\(10 \\times 9\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Number of dishes on the menu\nnum_dishes = 10\n\n# Number of different combinations of meals\ncombinations = num_dishes * (num_dishes - 1)\nprint(combinations)\n```\n```output\n90\n```\nThe total number of differ