ression = sp.simplify(expression)\n\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\ntan(pi/18)**2 + tan(pi/9)**2 + 3 + cot(pi/9)**2\n```\nThe output from the code indicates that the expression has not been fully simplified to a numerical value. To get the numerical value, we need to evaluate the trigonometric functions numerically. Let's modify the code to evaluate the expression numerically.\n\nHere is the updated code:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles in degrees\nangle1 = 10\nangle2 = 20\nangle3 = 40\n\n# Convert degrees to radians\nangle1_rad = sp.rad(angle1)\nangle2_rad = sp.rad(angle2)\nangle3_rad = sp.rad(angle3)\n\n# Define the trigonometric functions\ntan_10 = sp.tan(angle1_rad)\ncot_20 = 1 / sp.tan(angle2_rad)\ncot_40 = 1 / sp.tan(angle3_rad)\n\n# Compute the expression\nexpression = 3 + tan_10**2 + cot_20**2 + cot_40**2\n\n# Evaluate the expression numerically\nnumerical_value = expression.evalf()\n\nprint(numerical_value)\n```\n```output\n10.0000000000000\n```\nThe numerical evaluation of the expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}\\) is \\(10\\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]", "To solve the problem, we need to compute the expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}\\). Let's break it down step by step.\n\n1. First, recall that \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 x} = \\sec^2 x\\). So, the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ + \\csc^2 20^\\circ + \\csc^2 40^\\circ.\n   \\]\n2. We know the identity \\(\\csc^2 x = 1 + \\cot^2 x\\). So, we can rewrite the expression as:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ + (1 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ) + (1 + \\cot^2 40^\\circ).\n   \\]\n3. Simplifying further, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ + 2 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ + \\cot^2 40^\\circ.\n   \\]\n4. We also know that \\(\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\\). So, we can rewrite \\(\\sec^2 10^\\circ\\) as \\(1 + \\tan^2