dians\nangles_rad = [math.radians(angle) for angle in angles]\n\n# Calculate the tangent squared of each angle\ntan_squared_values = [math.tan(angle)**2 for angle in angles_rad]\n\n# Sum the tangent squared values and add 3\nresult = 3 + sum(tan_squared_values)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n17.000000000000004\n```\nThe computed value of the expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}\\) is approximately 17. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{17}\n\\]", "To solve the given expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 10^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 20^\\circ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 40^\\circ}\\), we need to use trigonometric identities and properties. Let's break it down step by step.\n\n1. We know that \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos^2 x} = \\sec^2 x\\). So the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ + \\csc^2 20^\\circ + \\csc^2 40^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n2. Recall the identity \\(\\csc^2 x = 1 + \\cot^2 x\\). So we can rewrite the expression as:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ + (1 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ) + (1 + \\cot^2 40^\\circ).\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ + 2 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ + \\cot^2 40^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n3. We know that \\(\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\\). So we can rewrite \\(\\sec^2 10^\\circ\\) as:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ = 1 + \\tan^2 10^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   Substituting this into the expression, we get:\n   \\[\n   1 + \\tan^2 10^\\circ + 2 + \\cot^2 20^\\circ + \\cot^2 40^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further, we get:\n   \\[\n   3 + \\tan^2 10^\\circ + \\cot^2 20^\\circ + \\cot^2 40^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n4. Now we need to evaluate \\(\\tan^2 10^\\circ + \\cot^2 20^\\circ + \\cot^2 40^\\circ\\). We can use the fact that \\(\\cot x = \\frac{1}{\\tan x}\\). So \\(\\cot^2 20^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 20^\\circ}\\) and \\(\\cot^2 40^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 40^\\circ}\\). Therefore, we need to evaluate:\n   \\[\n   \\tan^2 10^\\circ + \\frac{1}{\\tan^2 20^\\circ} 