ies:**\n   The expression can be written as:\n   \\[\n   \\sec^2 10^\\circ + \\csc^2 20^\\circ + \\csc^2 40^\\circ\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\sec x = \\frac{1}{\\cos x}\\) and \\(\\csc x = \\frac{1}{\\sin x}\\).\n\n2. **Use Known Trigonometric Values and Identities:**\n   We know that \\(\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\\) and \\(\\csc^2 x = 1 + \\cot^2 x\\). However, for this specific problem, we can use the fact that \\(\\csc^2 x = \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 x}\\) and \\(\\sec^2 x = \\frac{1}{\\cos^2 x}\\).\n\n3. **Simplify the Expression:**\n   We can use the known values of trigonometric functions at specific angles to simplify the expression. For this, we can use the fact that \\(\\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\\), and other known values.\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to get the exact value of the expression.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles in degrees\nangle1 = 10\nangle2 = 20\nangle3 = 40\n\n# Convert degrees to radians\nangle1_rad = sp.rad(angle1)\nangle2_rad = sp.rad(angle2)\nangle3_rad = sp.rad(angle3)\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = 1/sp.cos(angle1_rad)**2 + 1/sp.sin(angle2_rad)**2 + 1/sp.sin(angle3_rad)**2\n\n# Simplify the expression\nsimplified_expression = sp.simplify(expression)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(simplified_expression)\n```\n```output\ncos(2*pi/9)**(-2) + cos(pi/9)**(-2) + 4\n```\nThe output from the code simplifies the expression but doesn't give us a numerical value. To get the numerical value, we can evaluate the expression further. Let's evaluate the expression to get the final answer.\n\nHere is the updated Python code to evaluate the expression numerically:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles in degrees\nangle1 = 10\nangle2 = 20\nangle3 = 40\n\n# Convert degrees to radians\nangle1_rad = sp.rad(angle1)\nangle2_rad = sp.rad(angle2)\nangle3_rad = sp.rad(angle3)\n\n# Define the expression\nexpression = 1/sp.cos(angle1_rad)**2 + 1/sp.sin(angle2_rad)**2 + 1/sp.sin(angle3_rad)**2\n\n# Simplify 