d \\(\\lambda_2\\) are roots of unity, specifically \\(\\lambda_1 = e^{2\\pi i / 3}\\) and \\(\\lambda_2 = e^{-2\\pi i / 3}\\). This means that \\(\\lambda_1^3 = 1\\) and \\(\\lambda_2^3 = 1\\). Therefore, \\(\\lambda_1^{27} = (\\lambda_1^3)^9 = 1^9 = 1\\) and \\(\\lambda_2^{27} = 1\\). Similarly, \\(\\lambda_1^{31} = \\lambda_1^{3 \\cdot 10 + 1} = \\lambda_1 = e^{2\\pi i / 3}\\) and \\(\\lambda_2^{31} = \\lambda_2 = e^{-2\\pi i / 3}\\). Finally, \\(\\lambda_1^{40} = \\lambda_1^{3 \\cdot 13 + 1} = \\lambda_1 = e^{2\\pi i / 3}\\) and \\(\\lambda_2^{40} = \\lambda_2 = e^{-2\\pi i / 3}\\).\n\nSo, \\(\\mathbf{A}^{27} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{27} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{I} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\\), \\(\\mathbf{A}^{31} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{31} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{P} \\begin{pmatrix} \\lambda_1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda_2 \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{A}^{31} = \\mathbf{A}\\).\n\nTherefore, \\(\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{I} + \\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I} + 2\\mathbf{A}\\).\n\nNow we can compute:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{I} + 2\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 6 & 14 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the matrix A\nA = np.array([[3, 7], [-1, -2]])\n\n# Compute A^27, A^31, and A^40 using matrix power\nA_27 = np.linalg.matrix_power(A, 27)\nA_31 = np.linalg.matrix_power(A, 31)\nA_40 = np.linalg.matrix_power(A, 40)\n\n# Compute the sum A^27 + A^31 + A^40\nresult = A_27 + A_31 + A_40\n\n# Print the result\nprint(result)\n```", "To compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$, we need to understand the behavior of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$. We can use the concept of diagonalization to simplify the computation of high powers of the matrix.\n\nFirst, we need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$. The eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of $\\mathbf{A}$ are the roots of the characteristic polynomial, which is given by the determinant of $\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}$, where $\\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix.\n\nThe characteristic polynomial of $\\mathbf{A}$ is:\n\\[\n\\text{det}(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = \\text{det}\\left(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} - \\lambda \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\right) = \\text{det}\\left(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix}\\right) = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) + 7 = \\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1.\n\\]\n\nThe roots of the characteristic polynomial $\\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1 = 0$ are the eigenvalues. Using the quadratic formula $\\lambda = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, we get:\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSo the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. These eigenvalues are complex conjugates.\n\nSince the eigenvalues are complex, the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ is not diagonalizable over the real numbers. However, we can still use the fact that $\\mathbf{A}$ satisfies its own characteristic equation, i.e., $\\mathbf{A}^2 - \\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{0}$. This means $\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}$. We can use this relation to express higher powers of $\\mathbf{A}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{I}$.\n\nLet's compute $\\mathbf{A}^3$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} (\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}) = \\mathbf{A}^2 - \\mathbf{A} = (\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}) - \\mathbf{A} = -\\mathbf{I}.\n\\]\nThus, we have $\\mathbf{A}^3 = -\\mathbf{I}$. This implies that $\\mathbf{A}^6 = (\\mathbf{A}^3)^2 = (-\\mathbf{I})^2 = \\mathbf{I}$. Therefore, any power of $\\mathbf{A}$ that is a multiple of 6 will be $\\mathbf{I}$.\n\nNow we can compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} = (\\mathbf{A}^6)^4 \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{I}^4 \\cdot (-\\mathbf{I}) = -\\mathbf{I},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{31} = (\\mathbf{A}^6)^5 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I}^5 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{40} = (\\mathbf{A}^6)^6 \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^4 = \\mathbf{I}^6 \\cdot (\\mathbf{A}^6 \\cdot \\mathbf{A}^2) = \\mathbf{I} \\cdot (\\mathbf{I} \\cdot (\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I})) = \\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}.\n\\]\n\nSo we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = -\\mathbf{I} + \\mathbf{A} + (\\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I}) = -\\mathbf{I} + \\mathbf{A} + \\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{I} = 2\\mathbf{A} - 2\\mathbf{I}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute the values of $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{I}$:\n\\[\n2\\mathbf{A} - 2\\mathbf{I} = 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 & 14 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 14 \\\\ -2 & -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 14 \\\\ -2 & -6 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]", "To solve for $\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}$, we can use the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of matrix $\\mathbf{A}$. The steps are as follows:\n\n1. Find the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n2. Diagonalize the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ if possible.\n3. Use the diagonalization to compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$.\n4. Sum the matrices to get the final result.\n\nLet's start by finding the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nThe characteristic polynomial of $\\mathbf{A}$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{det}(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = \\begin{vmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{vmatrix} = (3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) + 7 = \\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1.\n\\]\nThe eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\\[\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1 = 0.\n\\]\nUsing the quadratic formula $\\lambda = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, we get:\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nSo the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nNext, we find the eigenvectors corresponding to these eigenvalues. For $\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFrom the first row, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)x + 7y = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{-14}{5 - i\\sqrt{3}} y = \\frac{-14(5 + i\\sqrt{3})}{28} y = \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} y.\n\\]\nSo an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ is $\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 - i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSimilarly, for $\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, we solve:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 7 \\\\ -1 & \\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFrom the first row, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)x + 7y = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{-14}{5 + i\\sqrt{3}} y = \\frac{-14(5 - i\\sqrt{3})}{28} y = \\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} y.\n\\]\nSo an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 + i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nNow we can diagonalize $\\mathbf{A}$ as $\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}$, where $\\mathbf{P}$ is the matrix of eigenvectors and $\\mathbf{D}$ is the diagonal matrix of eigenvalues:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 - i\\sqrt{3} & -5 + i\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nTo find $\\mathbf{A}^n$, we use:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^n = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^n \\mathbf{P}^{-1}.\n\\]\nWe need to compute $\\mathbf{D}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{D}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{D}^{40}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D}^{27} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D}^{31} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D}^{40} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSince $\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = -1$ and $\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3 = -1$, we have:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} = (-1)^9 = -1, \\quad \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{27} = (-1)^9 = -1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} = \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{30} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = (-1)^{10} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{31} = \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{30} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = (-1)^{10} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3\\right)^{13} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = (-1)^{13} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = -\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{40} = \\left(\\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^3\\right)^{13} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = (-1)^{13} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = -\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nSo we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D}^{27} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D}^{31} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{D}^{40} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can compute $\\mathbf{A}^{27}$, $\\mathbf{A}^{31}$, and $\\mathbf{A}^{40}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{27} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{P} \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = -\\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = -\\mathbf{I},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{31} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{31} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{P} \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{A},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{40} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D}^{40} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = \\mathbf{P} \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\mathbf{P}^{-1} = -\\mathbf{A}.\n\\]\nSo we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40} = -\\mathbf{I} + \\mathbf{A} - \\mathbf{A} = -\\mathbf{I}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the