at makes it easier to find the maximum value, such as a quadratic equation in the form \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\).\n3. Use calculus or the properties of quadratic functions to find the maximum value.\n\nLet's start by expanding the expression:\n\n\\[4(x + 7)(2 - x) = 4(x \\cdot 2 + x \\cdot (-x) + 7 \\cdot 2 + 7 \\cdot (-x)) = 4(2x - x^2 + 14 - 7x) = 4(-x^2 - 5x + 14) = -4x^2 - 20x + 56.\\]\n\nThe expression is now in the form of a quadratic function \\(f(x) = -4x^2 - 20x + 56\\). The maximum or minimum value of a quadratic function \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\) occurs at \\(x = -\\frac{b}{2a}\\). In this case, \\(a = -4\\), \\(b = -20\\), and \\(c = 56\\).\n\nSo, the value of \\(x\\) at which the maximum value occurs is:\n\n\\[x = -\\frac{-20}{2 \\cdot -4} = -\\frac{-20}{-8} = -\\frac{5}{2}.\\]\n\nNow we substitute \\(x = -\\frac{5}{2}\\) back into the original expression to find the maximum value:\n\n\\[4\\left(-\\frac{5}{2} + 7\\right)\\left(2 - \\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\right) = 4\\left(\\frac{-5 + 14}{2}\\right)\\left(2 + \\frac{5}{2}\\right) = 4\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) = 4 \\cdot \\frac{81}{4} = 81.\\]\n\nSo, the maximum value of the expression is \\(81\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variable and the expression\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\nexpression = 4 * (x + 7) * (2 - x)\r\n\r\n# Expand the expression\r\nexpanded_expression = sp.expand(expression)\r\n\r\n# Find the critical points by taking the derivative and setting it to zero\r\ncritical_points = sp.solve(sp.diff(expanded_expression, x), x)\r\n\r\n# Evaluate the expression at the critical points to find the maximum value\r\nmax_value = max(expanded_expression.subs(x, point) for point in critical_points)\r\n\r\nprint(max_value)\n```\n```output\n81\n```\nThe maximum value of the expression \\(4(x + 7)(2 - x)\\) is \\(\\boxed{81}\\).", "To find the maximum value of the expression \\(4(x + 7)(2 - x)\\), we can follow these steps:\n\