ays to select 6 cookies from a tray containing chocolate chip, oatmeal, and peanut butter cookies, where the order of selection does not matter and the cookies of the same type are indistinguishable. This is a classic example of a combinatorial problem that can be solved using the "stars and bars" theorem.\n\nThe "stars and bars" theorem states that the number of ways to distribute \\(n\\) indistinguishable items into \\(k\\) distinguishable bins is given by \\(\\binom{n+k-1}{k-1}\\). In this problem, we need to find the number of non-negative integer solutions to the equation \\(x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 6\\), where \\(x_1\\) is the number of chocolate chip cookies, \\(x_2\\) is the number of oatmeal cookies, and \\(x_3\\) is the number of peanut butter cookies. Here, \\(n = 6\\) (the total number of cookies to be selected) and \\(k = 3\\) (the number of different types of cookies).\n\nUsing the "stars and bars" theorem, the number of different assortments of six cookies that can be selected is given by \\(\\binom{6+3-1}{3-1} = \\binom{8}{2}\\).\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nn = 6\nk = 3\n\n# Calculate the number of combinations using the stars and bars method\nnumber_of_assortments = sp.binomial(n + k - 1, k - 1)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(number_of_assortments)\n```\n```output\n28\n```\nThe number of different assortments of six cookies that can be selected from a tray containing chocolate chip, oatmeal, and peanut butter cookies is \\(\\boxed{28}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we can use the "stars and bars" theorem from combinatorics. The problem is equivalent to finding the number of non-negative integer solutions to the equation \\(x + y + z = 6\\), where \\(x\\) is the number of chocolate chip cookies, \\(y\\) is the number of oatmeal cookies, and \\(z\\) is the number of peanut butter cookies.\n\nThe formula for the number of non-negative integer solutions to the equation \\(x_1 + x_2 +