. \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = \\frac{a^2 - 81}{2}\\)\n3. \\(r_1r_2r_3 = \\frac{c}{2}\\)\n\nSince \\(r_1, r_2,\\) and \\(r_3\\) are positive integers, \\(a\\) must be a positive integer, and \\(c\\) must be an even positive integer. Jon initially says there is more than one such polynomial, which means there are multiple sets of roots \\((r_1, r_2, r_3)\\) that satisfy the conditions for a given \\(a\\). After Steve reveals the value of \\(a\\), Jon still cannot determine the value of \\(c\\) uniquely, which means there are still two possible values of \\(c\\) for that \\(a\\).\n\nLet\'s write a Python script to find the possible values of \\(a\\) and \\(c\\):\n\n1. Iterate over possible values of \\(a\\).\n2. For each \\(a\\), find all sets of positive integers \\((r_1, r_2, r_3)\\) that satisfy \\(r_1 + r_2 + r_3 = a\\) and \\(r_1r_2 + r_2r_3 + r_3r_1 = \\frac{a^2 - 81}{2}\\).\n3. For each valid set of roots, compute \\(c = 2r_1r_2r_3\\).\n4. Check if there are exactly two different values of \\(c\\) for the given \\(a\\).\n5. Sum the two possible values of \\(c\\).\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python:\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations_with_replacement\n\ndef find_possible_c_values():\n    possible_c_values = {}\n    \n    # Iterate over possible values of a\n    for a in range(1, 100):  # We assume a reasonable range for a\n        c_values = set()\n        # Iterate over possible values of r1, r2, r3\n        for r1 in range(1, a):\n            for r2 in range(r1, a - r1 + 1):\n                r3 = a - r1 - r2\n                if r3 >= r2 and r3 > 0:\n                    if r1 * r2 + r2 * r3 + r3 * r1 == (a**2 - 81) / 2:\n                        c = 2 * r1 * r2 * r3\n                        c_values.add(c)\n        \n        if len(c_values) == 2:\n            possible_c_values[a] = c_values\n    \n    return possible_c_values\n\npossible_c_values = find_possible_c_values()\nif possible_c_values:\n    sum_of_c_values = sum(possible_c_values[a] for a