2 - 81}{2} \\) is an integer, which implies \\( a^2 - 81 \\) must be even. Therefore, \\( a \\) must be odd.\n\n### Step 2: Test small odd values of \\( a \\)\n\nWe will test small odd values of \\( a \\) to find those that yield more than one set of positive integer roots.\n\n#### Case \\( a = 9 \\)\n\\[\nr + s + t = 9\n\\]\n\\[\nrs + rt + st = \\frac{9^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{81 - 81}{2} = 0\n\\]\nThis is not possible since \\( r, s, \\) and \\( t \\) are positive integers.\n\n#### Case \\( a = 11 \\)\n\\[\nr + s + t = 11\n\\]\n\\[\nrs + rt + st = \\frac{11^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{121 - 81}{2} = 20\n\\]\nWe need to find positive integers \\( r, s, \\) and \\( t \\) such that:\n\\[\nr + s + t = 11 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad rs + rt + st = 20\n\\]\nTesting possible combinations:\n- \\( (1, 2, 8) \\): \\( 1 + 2 + 8 = 11 \\) and \\( 1 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 8 + 2 \\cdot 8 = 2 + 8 + 16 = 26 \\) (not valid)\n- \\( (1, 3, 7) \\): \\( 1 + 3 + 7 = 11 \\) and \\( 1 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 7 + 3 \\cdot 7 = 3 + 7 + 21 = 31 \\) (not valid)\n- \\( (1, 4, 6) \\): \\( 1 + 4 + 6 = 11 \\) and \\( 1 \\cdot 4 + 1 \\cdot 6 + 4 \\cdot 6 = 4 + 6 + 24 = 34 \\) (not valid)\n- \\( (2, 3, 6) \\): \\( 2 + 3 + 6 = 11 \\) and \\( 2 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 6 + 3 \\cdot 6 = 6 + 12 + 18 = 36 \\) (not valid)\n- \\( (2, 4, 5) \\): \\( 2 + 4 + 5 = 11 \\) and \\( 2 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 8 + 10 + 20 = 38 \\) (not valid)\n\n#### Case \\( a = 13 \\)\n\\[\nr + s + t = 13\n\\]\n\\[\nrs + rt + st = \\frac{13^2 - 81}{2} = \\frac{169 - 81}{2} = 44\n\\]\nWe need to find positive integers \\( r, s, \\) and \\( t \\) such that:\n\\[\nr + s + t = 13 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad rs + rt + st = 44\n\\]\nTesting possible combinations:\n- \\( (1, 4, 8) \\): \\( 1 + 4 + 8 = 13 \\) and \\( 1 \\cdot 4 + 1 \\cdot 8 + 4 \\cdot 8 = 4 + 8 + 32 = 44 \\) (valid)\n- \\( (2, 3, 8) \\): \\( 2 + 3 + 8 = 13 \\) and \\( 2 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 8 + 3 \\cdot 8 = 6 + 16 + 24 = 46 \\) (not valid)\n- \\( (2, 4, 7) \\): \\( 2 + 4 + 7 = 13 \\) and