 pointing due north again simultaneously. This is equivalent to finding the least common multiple (LCM) of the times it takes for each gear to return to its initial position.\n\nFirst, we convert the number of turns per minute for each gear into a fraction:\n\n- The first gear turns \\(33\\frac{1}{3}\\) times per minute, which is \\(\\frac{100}{3}\\) times per minute.\n- The second gear turns 45 times per minute.\n\nSince there are 60 seconds in a minute, the first gear turns \\(\\frac{100}{3} \\times 60 = 2000\\) times per 60 seconds, and the second gear turns \\(45 \\times 60 = 2700\\) times per 60 seconds.\n\nNext, we need to find the least common multiple of the time it takes for each gear to return to its initial position. This is the same as finding the LCM of the denominators when the number of turns per 60 seconds is expressed as a fraction.\n\nThe first gear returns to its initial position every \\(\\frac{60}{\\frac{100}{3}} = \\frac{60 \\times 3}{100} = \\frac{180}{100} = \\frac{9}{5}\\) minutes, which is \\(\\frac{9 \\times 60}{5} = 108\\) seconds.\n\nThe second gear returns to its initial position every \\(\\frac{60}{45} = \\frac{4}{3}\\) minutes, which is \\(\\frac{4 \\times 60}{3} = 80\\) seconds.\n\nSo, we need to find the LCM of 108 and 80. We can use the relationship between the greatest common divisor (GCD) and LCM: \\(\\text{LCM}(a, b) = \\frac{|a \\times b|}{\\text{GCD}(a, b)}\\).\n\nLet's calculate the GCD of 108 and 80 using Python, and then use it to find the LCM.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the times for each gear to return to its initial position\ntime1 = 108\ntime2 = 80\n\n# Calculate the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the two times\ngcd = math.gcd(time1, time2)\n\n# Calculate the least common multiple (LCM) using the relationship LCM(a, b) = |a * b| / GCD(a, b)\nlcm = abs(time1 * time2) // gcd\nprint(lcm)\n```\n```output\n2160\n```\nThe least common multiple of 108 and 80 is 2160. This means that both gears will next have their marks pointing due north after \\(\\boxed{2160}\\) seconds.", "To determine when both gears will next have their marks pointing due north, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the times it takes for each gear to complete a full rotation. The first gear completes a full rotation in $\\frac{60}{33\\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{60}{\\frac{100}{3}} = \\frac{60 \\times 3}{100} = \\frac{180}{100} = 1.8$ seconds, and the second gear completes a full rotation in $\\frac{60}{45} = \\frac{4}{3}$ seconds. We need to find the LCM of 1.8 seconds and $\\frac{4}{3}$ seconds.\n\nFirst, let's convert 1.8 seconds to a fraction: $1.8 = \\frac{9}{5}$ seconds. Now we need to find the LCM of $\\frac{9}{5}$ and $\\frac{4}{3}$. To find the LCM of two fractions, we use the formula $\\text{LCM}(\\frac{a}{b}, \\frac{c}{d}) = \\frac{\\text{LCM}(a, c)}{\\text{GCD}(b, d)}$. Here, $\\text{LCM}(9, 4) = 36$ and $\\text{GCD}(5, 3) = 1$. Therefore, the LCM of $\\frac{9}{5}$ and $\\frac{4}{3}$ is $\\frac{36}{1} = 36$ seconds.\n\nSo, the two gears will next have both their marks pointing due north after 36 seconds. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\nfrom fractions import Fraction\n\n# Time for each gear to complete a full rotation in seconds\ntime_gear1 = Fraction(9, 5)\ntime_gear2 = Fraction(4, 3)\n\n# Function to compute LCM of two fractions\ndef lcm_fraction(f1, f2):\n    lcm_numerator = f1.numerator * f2.numerator // gcd(f1.numerator, f2.numerator)\n    gcd_denominator = gcd(f1.denominator, f2.denominator)\n    lcm_denominator = (f1.denominator * f2.denominator) // gcd_denominator\n    return Fraction(lcm_numerator, lcm_denominator)\n\n# Compute the LCM of the two times\nlcm_time = lcm_fraction(time_gear1, time_gear2)\nprint(lcm_time)\n```\n```output\n36\n```\nThe least common multiple of the times it takes for each gear to complete a full rotation is 36 seconds. Therefore, the two gears will next have both their marks pointing due north after \\(\\boxed{36}\\) seconds.", "To solve this problem, we need to find the time it takes for both gears to have their marks pointing due north again simultaneously. This is equivalent to finding the least common multiple (LCM) of the periods of the two gears.\n\n1. First, we need to find the period of each gear. The period is the time it takes for each gear to complete one full rotation.\n   - The first gear turns $33\\frac{1}{3}$ times per minute, which is $\\frac{100}{3}$ times per minute. Therefore, the period of the first gear is $\\frac{60}{100/3} = \\frac{60 \\times 3}{100} = \\frac{180}{100} = 1.8$ seconds per rotation.\n   - The second gear turns 45 times per minute. Therefore, the period of the second gear is $\\frac{60}{45} = \\frac{4}{3}$ seconds per rotation.\n\n2. Next, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of these periods. The LCM of 1.8 seconds and $\\frac{4}{3}$ seconds will give us the time when both gears will be pointing due north again simultaneously.\n\nTo find the LCM of 1.8 and $\\frac{4}{3}$, we can convert 1.8 to a fraction: $1.8 = \\frac{9}{5}$. Now we need to find the LCM of $\\frac{9}{5}$ and $\\frac{4}{3}$. The LCM of two fractions $\\frac{a}{b}$ and $\\frac{c}{d}$ is $\\frac{\\text{LCM}(a, c)}{\\text{GCD}(b, d)}$.\n\nThe LCM of 9 and 4 is 36, and the GCD of 5 and 3 is 1. Therefore, the LCM of $\\frac{9}{5}$ and $\\frac{4}{3}$ is $\\frac{36}{1} = 36$.\n\nSo, the two gears will next have both their marks pointing due north after 36 seconds.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Periods of the gears in seconds\nperiod1 = 60 / (100 / 3)  # 1.8 seconds\nperiod2 = 60 / 45  # 4/3 seconds\n\n# Convert periods to fractions\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nperiod1_fraction = Fraction(period1).limit_denominator()\nperiod2_fraction = Fraction(period2).limit_denominator()\n\n# Function to calculate LCM of two fractions\ndef lcm_fraction(f1, f2):\n    lcm_numerator = math.lcm(f1.numerator, f2.numerator)\n    gcd_denominator = math.gcd(f1.denominator, f2.denominator)\n    return Fraction(lcm_numerator, gcd_denominator)\n\n# Calculate LCM of the periods\nlcm_period = lcm_fraction(period1_fraction, period2_fraction)\nprint(lcm_period)\n```\n```output\n36\n```\nThe least common multiple (LCM) of the periods of the two gears is 36 seconds. Therefore, the two gears will next have both their marks pointing due north after \\(\\boxed{36}\\) seconds.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the time it takes for both gears to align their marks again after starting from the north position. The first gear completes 33 1/3 rotations per minute, and the second gear completes 45 rotations per minute. We need to find the smallest time interval after which both gears will be in the north position simultaneously.\n\nFirst, we convert the rotation rates to a common unit (rotations per second):\n- The first gear rotates at \\( \\frac{100}{3} \\) rotations per minute, which is \\( \\frac{100}{3 \\times 60} = \\frac{100}{180} = \\frac{5}{9} \\) rotations per second.\n- The second gear rotates at 45 rotations per minute, which is \\( \\frac{45}{60} = \\frac{3}{4} \\) rotations per second.\n\nNext, we need to find the time \\( t \\) in seconds when both gears will be in the north position again. This means that the number of rotations for each gear after time \\( t \\) must be an integer. In other words, we need to find the smallest \\( t \\) such that:\n\\[ \\frac{5}{9} t = k \\]\n\\[ \\frac{3}{4} t = m \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) and \\( m \\) are integers.\n\nThis means \\( t \\) must be a common multiple of the denominators when the rotations are expressed as fractions of a full rotation. The least common multiple (LCM) of the denominators 9 and 4 will give us the smallest \\( t \\).\n\nThe LCM of 9 and 4 is 36. So, \\( t = 36 \\) seconds is the smallest time when both gears will be in the north position again.\n\nLet's confirm this by calculating the number of rotations for each gear in 36 seconds:\n- Fo