ersection of these intervals.\n\nLet's do this step-by-step.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, solve, Interval\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\np = symbols('p')\r\n\r\n# Define the inequalities\r\ninequality1 = 0 >= 54*p - 144\r\ninequality2 = 0 > 12 - 20*p\r\n\r\n# Solve the inequalities\r\nsolution1 = solve(inequality1, p)\r\nsolution2 = solve(inequality2, p)\r\n\r\n# Convert the solutions to Interval objects\r\ninterval1 = Interval(-float('inf'), solution1.rhs, right_open=False)\r\ninterval2 = Interval(solution2.lhs, float('inf'), left_open=True)\r\n\r\n# Find the intersection of the intervals\r\nsolution = interval1.intersect(interval2)\r\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\nAttributeError: 'And' object has no attribute 'rhs'\n```\n\nReach max function call limit.", "To solve the problem, we need to find the values of \\( p \\) that satisfy both inequalities \\( 0 \\ge 54p - 144 \\) and \\( 0 > 12 - 20p \\). Let's solve each inequality separately and then find the intersection of the solutions.\n\n1. Solve the inequality \\( 0 \\ge 54p - 144 \\):\n\\[ 0 \\ge 54p - 144 \\]\n\\[ 144 \\ge 54p \\]\n\\[ \\frac{144}{54} \\ge p \\]\n\\[ \\frac{8}{3} \\ge p \\]\n\\[ p \\le \\frac{8}{3} \\]\n\n2. Solve the inequality \\( 0 > 12 - 20p \\):\n\\[ 0 > 12 - 20p \\]\n\\[ -12 > -20p \\]\n\\[ \\frac{12}{20} < p \\]\n\\[ \\frac{3}{5} < p \\]\n\\[ p > \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the intersection of the solutions \\( p \\le \\frac{8}{3} \\) and \\( p > \\frac{3}{5} \\). The intersection of these intervals is:\n\\[ \\frac{3}{5} < p \\le \\frac{8}{3} \\]\n\nSo, the solution in interval notation is:\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{8}{3} \\right] \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, solve, S\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\np = symbols('p')\r\n\r\n# Define the inequalities\r\ninequality1 = 0 >= 54*p - 144\r\ninequality2 = 0 > 12 - 20*p\r\n\r\n# Solve the inequalities\r\nsolution1 = solve(inequality1, p)\r\n