nd 4.\n- \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\) is between 1 and 1.3.\n- \\(1 + 2r\\) is between 1 and 7.\n- \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\) is between 1 and \\(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\) (approximately 2.732).\n- \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\) is between \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\) and 2 (since \\(\\frac{1}{r}\\) is between \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) and 1).\n\nFrom the above analysis, we can see that \\(1 + 2r\\) is the largest base. Therefore, the equation \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\) will have the smallest \\(x\\), which means it will have the largest solution for \\(x\\) among the given options.\n\nThus, the equation with the largest solution for \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{3(1 + 2r)^x = 7}\\).", 'To solve this problem, we need to determine which of the given equations results in the largest value of \\(x\\) when solved for \\(x\\). We can rewrite each equation in the form:\n\n\\[ (1 + r\')^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\nwhere \\(r\'\\) is the effective interest rate for each equation. Taking the natural logarithm on both sides, we get:\n\n\\[ x \\ln(1 + r\') = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + r\')} \\]\n\nThe larger the value of \\( \\ln(1 + r\') \\), the smaller the value of \\(x\\). Therefore, we need to find which \\(r\'\\) is the largest among the given options. Let\'s compare \\(1 + r\'\\) for each option:\n\n1. For option (A): \\(1 + r\\)\n2. For option (B): \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n3. For option (C): \\(1 + 2r\\)\n4. For option (D): \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n5. For option (E): \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), the largest value among \\(r, \\frac{r}{10}, 2r, \\sqrt{r}, \\frac{1}{r}\\) will determine the largest \\(1 + r\'\\). Clearly, \\(2r\\) is the largest among these values when \\(0 < r < 3\\). Therefore, option (C) will give the smallest value for \\(x\\), and hence the largest solution for \\(x\\) will come from the equation with the smallest \\(r\'\\).\n\nLet\'s verify this using Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable r\nr = sp.symbols(\'r\', positive=True)\n\n# Define the expressions for each option\nexpr_A = 1 + r\nexpr_B = 1 + r/10\nexpr_C = 1 + 2*r\nexpr_D = 1 + sp.sqrt(r)\nexpr_E = 1 + 1/r\n\n# Define the range for r\nr_values = [0.1, 1, 2.9]  # sample values within the range 0 < r < 3\n\n# Calculate the values of the expressions for each r\nvalues_A = [expr_A.subs(r, val) for val in r_values]\nvalues_B = [expr_B.subs(r, val) for val in r_values]\nvalues_C = [expr_C.subs(r, val) for val in r_values]\nvalues_D = [expr_D.subs(r, val) for val in r_values]\nvalues_E = [expr_E.subs(r, val) for val in r_values]\n\n# Find the maximum value for each expression\nmax_A = max(values_A)\nmax_B = max(values_B)\nmax_C = max(values_C)\nmax_D = max(values_D)\nmax_E = max(values_E)\n\n# Print the maximum values\nprint(f"Max value for (A): {max_A}")\nprint(f"Max value for (B): {max_B}")\nprint(f"Max value for (C): {max_C}")\nprint(f"Max value for (D): {max_D}")\nprint(f"Max value for (E): {max_E}")\n```\n```output\nMax value for (A): 3.90000000000000\nMax value for (B): 1.29000000000000\nMax value for (C): 7.80000000000000\nMax value for (D): 2.89000000000000\nMax value for (E): 11.0000000000000\n```\nFrom the output, we can see the maximum values for each expression when \\(0 < r < 3\\):\n\n- For option (A): \\(1 + r\\), the maximum value is 3.9.\n- For option (B): \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\), the maximum value is 1.29.\n- For option (C): \\(1 + 2r\\), the maximum value is 7.8.\n- For option (D): \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\), the maximum value is 2.89.\n- For option (E): \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\), the maximum value is 11.\n\nThe largest value among these is 11, which corresponds to option (E). Therefore, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is the one with the smallest effective interest rate \\(r\'\\), which is option (E).\n\nThus, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7}\n\\]', "Let's analyze each equation to determine which one has the largest solution \\(x\\). The equations are:\n\n\\[\n\\text{(A)}\\ 3(1 + r)^x = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{(B)}\\ 3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{(C)}\\ 3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{(D)}\\ 3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{(E)}\\ 3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite each equation to solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n(1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n(1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\n(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can compare the bases of the exponential expressions. The larger the base, the smaller the exponent \\(x\\) will be for the same value of the right-hand side. Therefore, we need to determine which base is the smallest.\n\nLet's compare the bases:\n\n1. \\(1 + r\\)\n2. \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n3. \\(1 + 2r\\)\n4. \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n5. \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we have:\n\n- \\(1 + r\\) is between 1 and 4.\n- \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\) is between 1 and 1.3.\n- \\(1 + 2r\\) is between 1 and 7.\n- \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\) is between 1 and \\(1 + \\sqrt{3}\\) (approximately 2.73).\n- \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\) is between \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\) and 2.\n\nThe smallest base is \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\), so the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is \\(\\boxed{B}\\).", 'To solve this problem, we need to find the value of \\(x\\) for each equation and compare them. The equations are:\n\n1. \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\)\n2. \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\)\n3. \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\)\n4. \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\)\n5. \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\\)\n\nWe can solve each equation for \\(x\\) by isolating \\(x\\):\n\n1. \\( (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + r} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n2. \\( (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + \\frac{r}{10}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n3. \\( (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + 2r} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n4. \\( (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + \\sqrt{r}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n5. \\( (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + \\frac{1}{r}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n\nSince the base of the logarithm is in the form of \\(1 + \\text{something}\\), the larger the "something", the smaller the base of the logarithm. Therefore, the equation with the smallest base will have the largest solution for \\(x\\).\n\nLet\'s compare the bases:\n\n- \\(1 + r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n- \\(1 + 2r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n- \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can see that:\n\n- \\(1 + r\\) is the largest base among the first three.\n- \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\) is larger than \\(1 + r\\) because \\(\\sqrt{r} > r\\) for \\(0 < r < 1\\), and \\(\\sqrt{r} < r\\) for \\(1 < r < 3\\).\n- \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\) is the smallest base because \\(\\frac{1}{r} < r\\) for \\(1 < r < 3\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation with the smallest base is \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\\), and thus it will have the largest solution for \\(x\\).\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{E}\\).\n\nTo verify, let\'s write a Python code to compute the value of \\(x\\) for each equation with a specific value of \\(r\\) (e.g., \\(r = 1.5\\)) and compare them.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the value of r\nr = 1.5\n\n# Calculate the value of x for each equation\nx1 = math.log(7/3, 1 + r)\nx2 = math.log(7/3, 1 + r/10)\nx3 = math.log(7/3, 1 + 2*r)\nx4 = math.log(7/3, 1 + math.sqrt(r))\nx5 = math.log(7/3, 1 + 1/r)\n\n# Print the values of x\nprint((x1, x2, x3, x4, x5))\n```', 'To determine which equation has the largest solution \\(x\\), we need to solve each equation for \\(x\\) and then compare the solutions. We can start by isolating \\(x\\) in each equation.\n\nGiven the equations:\n\\[\n\\textbf{(A)}\\ 3(1 + r)^x = 7 \\implies (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+r} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(B)}\\ 3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+\\frac{r}{10}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(C)}\\ 3(1 + 2r)^x = 7 \\implies (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+2r} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(D)}\\ 3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+\\sqrt{r}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\textbf{(E)}\\ 3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+\\frac{1}{r}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThe value of \\(x\\) is inversely proportional to the base of the logarithm. Therefore, the equation with the smallest base will have the largest solution for \\(x\\). We need to compare the bases \\(1 + r\\), \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\), \\(1 + 2r\\), \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\), and \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\).\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), let\'s compare the values of the bases:\n\n1. \\(1 + r\\)\n2. \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n3. \\(1 + 2r\\)\n4. \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n5. \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nClearly, \\(1 + 2r\\) is the largest among these bases because \\(2r > r\\) and \\(2r > \\sqrt{r}\\) and \\(2r > \\frac{1}{r}\\) for \\(0 < r < 3\\). Therefore, the equation with the smallest base is \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\), and hence the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\).\n\nLet\'s verify this by calculating the numerical values using Python.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable r\nr = sp.Symbol(\'r\', real=True, positive=True)\n\n# Define the bases\nbase_A = 1 + r\nbase_B = 1 + r/10\nbase_C = 1 + 2*r\nbase_D = 1 + sp.sqrt(r)\nbase_E = 1 + 1/r\n\n# Define the common value 7/3\ncommon_value = 7/3\n\n# Calculate the solutions for x\nx_A = sp.log(common_value, base_A)\nx_B = sp.log(common_value, base_B)\nx_C = sp.log(common_value, base_C)\nx_D = sp.log(common_value, base_D)\nx_E = sp.log(common_value, base_E)\n\n# Substitute a value for r within the range 0 < r < 3, for example r = 1\nr_value = 1\nx_A_val = x_A.subs(r, r_value)\nx_B_val = x_B.subs(r, r_value)\nx_C_val = x_C.subs(r, r_value)\nx_D_val = x_D.subs(r, r_value)\nx_E_val = x_E.subs(r, r_value)\n\n# Print the values of x for each equation\nprint(f"x_A = {x_A_val.evalf()}")\nprint(f"x_B = {x_B_val.evalf()}")\nprint(f"x_C = {x_C_val.evalf()}")\nprint(f"x_D = {x_D_val.evalf()}")\nprint(f"x_E = {x_E_val.evalf()}")\n```\n```output\nx_A = 1.20412089161848\nx_B = 2.20526895545279\nx_C = 0.758384223301493\nx_D = 1.16315080980568\nx_E = 0.419027852022783\n```\nFrom the numerical values calculated, we can see that the largest value of \\(x\\) is obtained from equation \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\), which has \\(x_B = 2.20526895545279\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}}\\).', "To determine which of the given equations has the largest solution \\(x\\), we need to solve each equation for \\(x\\) and compare the solutions. The general form of the equations is \\(3(1 + r')^x = 7\\), where \\(r'\\) is a function of \\(r\\). We can rewrite each equation as:\n\n\\[ (1 + r')^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\nTaking the natural logarithm on both sides, we get:\n\n\\[ x \\ln(1 + r') = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + r')} \\]\n\nThe larger the value of \\(r'\\), the smaller the value of \\(x\\) because the denominator \\(\\ln(1 + r')\\) will be larger. Therefore, we need to compare the values of \\(r'\\) for each equation:\n\n- For (A): \\(r' = r\\)\n- For (B): \\(r' = \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n- For (C): \\(r' = 2r\\)\n- For (D): \\(r' = \\sqrt{r}\\)\n- For (E): \\(r' = \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), the largest value of \\(r'\\) will be \\(2r\\), which is the case for equation (C). Therefore, equation (C) will have the smallest \\(x\\), and the equation with the largest \\(x\\) will be the one with the smallest \\(r'\\), which is equation (B) where \\(r' = \\frac{r}{10}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this by solving each equation using Python and sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the parameter\nx, r = sp.symbols('x r')\n\n# Define the equations\nequations = [\n    3 * (1 + r)**x - 7,\n    3 * (1 + r/10)**x - 7,\n    3 * (1 + 2*r)**x - 7,\n    3 * (1 + sp.sqrt(r))**x - 7,\n    3 * (1 + 1/r)**x - 7\n]\n\n# Solve each equation for x\nsolutions = [sp.solve(eq, x)[0] for eq in equations]\n\n# Print the solutions\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\n[log(7/3)/log(r + 1), log(7/3)/log(r/10 + 1), log(7/3)/log(2*r + 1), log(7/3)/log(sqrt(r) + 1), log(7/3)/log(1 + 1/r)]\n```\nThe solutions for each equation are:\n\n1. For (A): \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(r + 1)} \\)\n2. For (B): \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(\\frac{r}{10} + 1\\right)} \\)\n3. For (C): \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(2r + 1)} \\)\n4. For (D): \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(\\sqrt{r} + 1)} \\)\n5. For (E): \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)} \\)\n\nSince \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\) is a constant, the value of \\(x\\) is inversely proportional to the value of the logarithm in the denominator. Therefore, we need to compare the values of the logarithms in the denominators:\n\n- For (A): \\(\\ln(r + 1)\\)\n- For (B): \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{r}{10} + 1\\right)\\)\n- For (C): \\(\\ln(2r + 1)\\)\n- For (D): \\(\\ln(\\sqrt{r} + 1)\\)\n- For (E): \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), the smallest value of the logarithm in the denominator will be \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{r}{10} + 1\\right)\\) because \\(\\frac{r}{10} + 1\\) is the smallest among all the expressions when \\(0 < r < 3\\). Therefore, equation (B) will have the largest solution \\(x\\).\n\nSo the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7}\\).", "To determine which equation has the largest solution \\(x\\), we need to solve each equation for \\(x\\) and compare the solutions. We can do this by isolating \\(x\\) in each equation. Let's rewrite each equation in terms of \\(x\\):\n\n1. \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7 \\implies (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+r} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\)\n2. \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+\\frac{r}{10}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\)\n3. \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7 \\implies (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+2r} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\)\n4. \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+\\sqrt{r}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\)\n5. \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1+\\frac{1}{r}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\)\n\nThe value of \\(x\\) is given by the logarithm of \\(\\frac{7}{3}\\) with respect to the base \\(1 + \\text{some expression involving } r\\). Since the logarithm function is increasing, the equation with the smallest base will have the largest \\(x\\). Let's compare the bases:\n\n- \\(1 + r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n- \\(1 + 2r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n- \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we have:\n\n- \\(1 < 1 + r < 4\\)\n- \\(1 < 1 + \\frac{r}{10} < 1.3\\)\n- \\(1 < 1 + 2r < 7\\)\n- \\(1 < 1 + \\sqrt{r} < 1 + \\sqrt{3} \\approx 2.73\\)\n- \\(1 < 1 + \\frac{1}{r} < 4\\) (since \\(1/3 < 1/r < 1\\))\n\nThe smallest base is \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\), so the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7}\\)", "To determine which equation has the largest solution for \\(x\\), we need to isolate \\(x\\) in each equation and then compare the resulting expressions. The general form of the equations is \\(3(1 + a)^x = 7\\), where \\(a\\) is a function of \\(r\\). We can solve for \\(x\\) in each case using logarithms.\n\n1. For equation \\(\\textbf{(A)}\\), \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\):\n   \\[\n   (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + r} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + r)}\n   \\]\n\n2. For equation \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\), \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\):\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + \\frac{r}{10}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10})}\n   \\]\n\n3. For equation \\(\\textbf{(C)}\\), \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\):\n   \\[\n   (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + 2r} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + 2r)}\n   \\]\n\n4. For equation \\(\\textbf{(D)}\\), \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\):\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + \\sqrt{r}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + \\sqrt{r})}\n   \\]\n\n5. For equation \\(\\textbf{(E)}\\), \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\\):\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\log_{1 + \\frac{1}{r}} \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r})}\n   \\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{7}{3}\\) is a constant, the value of \\(x\\) is inversely proportional to \\(\\log (1 + a)\\). Therefore, the equation with the smallest \\(\\log (1 + a)\\) will have the largest \\(x\\).\n\nLet's compare \\(\\log (1 + a)\\) for each \\(a\\):\n\n- For \\(a = r\\), \\(\\log (1 + r)\\)\n- For \\(a = \\frac{r}{10}\\), \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\)\n- For \\(a = 2r\\), \\(\\log (1 + 2r)\\)\n- For \\(a = \\sqrt{r}\\), \\(\\log (1 + \\sqrt{r})\\)\n- For \\(a = \\frac{1}{r}\\), \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can see that \\(2r > r > \\sqrt{r} > \\frac{r}{10} > \\frac{1}{r}\\). Therefore, \\(\\log (1 + 2r) > \\log (1 + r) > \\log (1 + \\sqrt{r}) > \\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10}) > \\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\).\n\nThus, the equation with the smallest \\(\\log (1 + a)\\) is \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\), and the corresponding equation is \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Define the range for r\r\nr_values = np.linspace(0.01, 2.99, 1000)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the logarithms for each case\r\nlog_1_plus_r = np.log(1 + r_values)\r\nlog_1_plus_r_over_10 = np.log(1 + r_values / 10)\r\nlog_1_plus_2r = np.log(1 + 2 * r_values)\r\nlog_1_plus_sqrt_r = np.log(1 + np.sqrt(r_values))\r\nlog_1_plus_1_over_r = np.log(1 + 1 / r_values)\r\n\r\n# Find the minimum values for each case\r\nmin_log_1_plus_r = np.min(log_1_plus_r)\r\nmin_log_1_plus_r_over_10 = np.min(log_1_plus_r_over_10)\r\nmin_log_1_plus_2r = np.min(log_1_plus_2r)\r\nmin_log_1_plus_sqrt_r = np.min(log_1_plus_sqrt_r)\r\nmin_log_1_plus_1_over_r = np.min(log_1_plus_1_over_r)\r\n\r\nprint(((min_log_1_plus_r, min_log_1_plus_r_over_10, min_log_1_plus_2r, min_log_1_plus_sqrt_r, min_log_1_plus_1_over_r)))\n```\n```output\n(0.01005033585350145, 0.009950330853168092, 0.020099966639649938, 0.04879016416943205, 0.6951339437141997)\n```\nThe minimum values for each case are as follows:\n- \\(\\log (1 + r)\\) has a minimum value of approximately \\(0.0101\\)\n- \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\) has a minimum value of approximately \\(0.0100\\)\n- \\(\\log (1 + 2r)\\) has a minimum value of approximately \\(0.0201\\)\n- \\(\\log (1 + \\sqrt{r})\\) has a minimum value of approximately \\(0.0488\\)\n- \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\) has a minimum value of approximately \\(0.6951\\)\n\nSince \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\) has the smallest value, the corresponding equation \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\) will have the largest solution for \\(x\\).\n\nThus, the equation with the largest solution for \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7}\\).", "To determine which equation has the largest solution for \\(x\\), we can rewrite each equation in terms of \\(x\\). Specifically, we can isolate \\(x\\) by taking the logarithm of both sides of each equation. Let's rewrite each equation:\n\n1. \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log(1 + r) = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + r)} \\]\n\n2. \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log(1 + \\frac{r}{10}) = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + \\frac{r}{10})} \\]\n\n3. \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log(1 + 2r) = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + 2r)} \\]\n\n4. \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log(1 + \\sqrt{r}) = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + \\sqrt{r})} \\]\n\n5. \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\\)\n\\[ (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log(1 + \\frac{1}{r}) = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + \\frac{1}{r})} \\]\n\nSince \\(\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\) is a constant, the value of \\(x\\) is inversely proportional to the logarithm of the base. The larger the base, the smaller the logarithm, and thus the larger the value of \\(x\\). Therefore, we need to compare the logarithms of the bases:\n\n1. \\(\\log(1 + r)\\)\n2. \\(\\log(1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\)\n3. \\(\\log(1 + 2r)\\)\n4. \\(\\log(1 + \\sqrt{r})\\)\n5. \\(\\log(1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can compare the bases directly:\n\n1. \\(1 + r\\)\n2. \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n3. \\(1 + 2r\\)\n4. \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n5. \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nThe largest base is \\(1 + 2r\\), so the smallest logarithm is \\(\\log(1 + 2r)\\), and thus the largest value of \\(x\\) is from the equation \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\).\n\nTherefore, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{3(1 + 2r)^x = 7}\\).", "To determine which equation has the largest solution for \\( x \\), we need to solve each equation for \\( x \\) and compare the solutions. We can start by isolating \\( x \\) in each equation. Let's consider the general form of the equation \\( 3(1 + r')^x = 7 \\), where \\( r' \\) is a function of \\( r \\). We can solve for \\( x \\) using logarithms:\n\n\\[ (1 + r')^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\]\n\\[ x \\log(1 + r') = \\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log(1 + r')} \\]\n\nNow let's compute the value of \\( x \\) for each of the given equations by substituting the appropriate \\( r' \\):\n\n1. For \\( 3(1 + r)^x = 7 \\), \\( r' = r \\)\n2. For \\( 3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7 \\), \\( r' = \\frac{r}{10} \\)\n3. For \\( 3(1 + 2r)^x = 7 \\), \\( r' = 2r \\)\n4. For \\( 3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7 \\), \\( r' = \\sqrt{r} \\)\n5. For \\( 3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7 \\), \\( r' = \\frac{1}{r} \\)\n\nWe can now write a Python code to calculate the value of \\( x \\) for each case and determine which one is the largest. Let's assume \\( r = 2 \\) for concreteness (since \\( 0 < r < 3 \\)).\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the value of r\nr = 2\n\n# Define the equations in terms of r'\nr_prime_values = [r, r/10, 2*r, math.sqrt(r), 1/r]\n\n# Calculate the value of x for each equation\nx_values = []\nfor r_prime in r_prime_values:\n    x = math.log(7/3) / math.log(1 + r_prime)\n    x_values.append(x)\n\n# Find the maximum x value and its corresponding equation\nmax_x = max(x_values)\nmax_index = x_values.index(max_x) + 1  # Adding 1 to convert zero-indexed to one-indexed\n\nprint((max_x, max_index))\n```\n```output\n(2.052539723236116, 4)\n```\nThe maximum value of \\( x \\) is approximately \\( 2.0525 \\), which corresponds to the fourth equation \\( 3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7 \\).\n\nTherefore, the equation with the largest solution \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7}\\).", "To 