:\n\n1. \\( (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\)\n2. \\( (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\)\n3. \\( (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\)\n4. \\( (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\)\n5. \\( (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\)\n\nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides of each equation, we get:\n\n1. \\( x \\ln(1 + r) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n2. \\( x \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n3. \\( x \\ln(1 + 2r) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n4. \\( x \\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r}) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n5. \\( x \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\)\n\nSolving for \\(x\\) in each equation, we get:\n\n1. \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + r)} \\)\n2. \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right)} \\)\n3. \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + 2r)} \\)\n4. \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})} \\)\n5. \\( x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)} \\)\n\nSince \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\) is a constant, the value of \\(x\\) is inversely proportional to the natural logarithm of the base. Therefore, we need to compare the natural logarithms of the bases:\n\n1. \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\)\n2. \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right)\\)\n3. \\(\\ln(1 + 2r)\\)\n4. \\(\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})\\)\n5. \\(\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can compare these logarithms by noting that the natural logarithm function is an increasing function. Therefore, the largest solution for \\(x\\) will correspond to the smallest base. Let's compare the bases:\n\n1. \\(1 + r\\)\n2. \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n3. \\(1 + 2r\\)\n4. \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n5. \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we have:\n\n- \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10} < 1 + r\\)\n- \\(1 + 2r > 1 + r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\sqrt{r} > 1 + r\\) (since \\(\\sqrt{r} > r\\) for