\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\nUsing the properties of the cross product, we know that:\n\\[\n2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}),\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}),\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.\n\\]\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can compute this step by step:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nAdding these together, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Given vectors\r\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\r\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\r\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\r\n\r\n# Compute the required cross product\r\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c - 2 * a_cross_b - 3 * a_cross_c\r\nprint(result)\n```", "To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and scalar multiplication. Specifically, we have:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of the cross product, we can simplify this expression:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = 2 \\cdot 3 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a})\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\]\n\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})\n\\]\n\nWe are given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of the cross product, we know that \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\). So:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute these values into the expression:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet's compute this step by step:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nAdding these together:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given cross products\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Compute the required cross product\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c + 2 * (-a_cross_b) - 3 * a_cross_c\nprint(result)\n```", "To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product to simplify this expression.\n\nThe cross product distributes over addition and scalar multiplication, so we have:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify each term using the given cross products and the properties of the cross product. Note that \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\) (the zero vector), and \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = -(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 (-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = -2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = -3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together, we get:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Compute the required cross product\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c - 2 * a_cross_b - 3 * a_cross_c\nprint(result)\n```", "To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product to simplify this expression:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} + 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\n\nWe know that the cross product of a vector with itself is zero, so \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\). Therefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) + 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\nWe are given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = - (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can compute the result by performing the scalar multiplication and vector addition:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\begin{pmatrix} -6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n-3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these vectors together:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Compute b_cross_a\nb_cross_a = -a_cross_b\n\n# Compute the final cross product\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c + 2 * b_cross_a - 3 * a_cross_c\nprint(result)\n```", "To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and subtraction, and the properties of the cross product of vectors. Specifically, we will use the following properties:\n\n1. \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\)\n2. \\((\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) \\times \\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\)\n3. \\((k\\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = k(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times (k\\mathbf{v})\\)\n4. \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0}\\)\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to compute:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property of the cross product, we can expand this as:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying each term:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) = -2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = -2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = -3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.\n\\]\n\nCombining these results, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 + 14 - 21 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Compute the required cross product\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c - 2 * a_cross_b - 3 * a_cross_c\n\nprint(result)\n```", "To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and the properties of the cross product with scalar multiplication. The cross product distributes over addition, so we have:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} + (2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property again, we get:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} = 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c} = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})\n\\]\n\nAnd,\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) - \\mathbf{0} = 2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a})\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a})\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n2 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) = -2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\n\\]\n\nSo, combining these results, we get:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute the given values:\n\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = -3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) = -2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nAdding these vectors together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 - 12 - 12 \\\\ -42 - 21 + 14 \\\\ 108 - 6 - 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ -49 \\\\ 96 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\na_cross_b = np.array([6, -7, 3])\na_cross_c = np.array([4, 7, 2])\nb_cross_c = np.array([1, -7, 18])\n\n# Calculate the required cross product\nresult = 6 * b_cross_c - 3 * a_cross_c - 2 * a_cross_b\nprint(result)\n```", 'To solve the problem, we need to compute the cross product \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\). We can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and scalar multiplication. The distributive property of the cross product states that for any vectors \\(\\mathbf{u}\\), \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{w}\\), and any scalar \\(k\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) \\times \\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\n\\]\n\\[\n(k \\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = k (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) = \\mathbf{u} \\times (k \\mathbf{v})\n\\]\n\nUsing these properties, we can expand the expression \\((2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a})\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying each term:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})\n\\]\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = -2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b})\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c}) = -3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c})\n\\]\n\\[\n(-\\mathbf{a}) \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0} \\quad \\text{(since the cross product of any vector with itself is the zero vector)}\n\\]\n\nSo the expression simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) 