g_range)\n```\n```output\n(-oo, 0)\n```\nThe range of the function \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) for \\( 0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ \\) is \\(\\boxed{(-\\infty, 0]}\\).", "To find the range of the function \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) for \\( 0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the function over this interval. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Determine the range of \\(\\sin x\\)**:\n   - For \\( 0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ \\), \\(\\sin x\\) ranges from \\(0\\) (exclusive) to \\(1\\) (inclusive). This is because \\(\\sin x\\) reaches its maximum value of \\(1\\) at \\(90^\\circ\\) and is positive in the first and second quadrants.\n\n2. **Determine the range of \\(\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\)**:\n   - Since \\(\\sin x\\) ranges from \\(0\\) (exclusive) to \\(1\\) (inclusive), \\(\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\) ranges from \\(0\\) (exclusive) to \\(1\\) (inclusive).\n\n3. **Determine the range of \\(\\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x})\\)**:\n   - The logarithm function \\(\\log_2 (z)\\) is defined for \\(z > 0\\). Since \\(\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\) ranges from \\(0\\) (exclusive) to \\(1\\) (inclusive), \\(\\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x})\\) ranges from \\(-\\infty\\) to \\(0\\) (inclusive). This is because \\(\\log_2 (1) = 0\\) and as \\(\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\) approaches \\(0\\) from the right, \\(\\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x})\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\).\n\nTherefore, the range of \\( y = \\log_2 (\\sqrt{\\sin x}) \\) for \\( 0^\\circ < x < 180^\\circ \\) is \\((-\\infty, 0]\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the function\ndef f(x):\n    return np.log2(np.sqrt(np.sin(np.deg2rad(x))))\n\n# Generate values of x from 0.01 to 179.99 degrees\nx_values = np.linspace(0.01, 179.99, 10000)\ny_values = f(x_values)\n\n# Determine the range of the function\ny_min = np.min(y_values)\ny_max = np.max(y_values)\n\nprint(((y_min, y_max)))\n```\n```output\n(-10.014856047562702, -0.0002095344702233994)\n```\nThe numerical results fr