tions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$. The operation is defined as $\\frac{m}{n}@\\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)$. Here, $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify $\\frac{21}{30}$ to its simplest form. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{21 \\div 3}{30 \\div 3} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute this back into the expression:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = 7 \\cdot 10 \\cdot \\frac{7}{10} = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21}$ is 49. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\r\n\r\n# Define the fractions\r\nm, n = 7, 30\r\np, q = 10, 21\r\n\r\n# Simplify q/n\r\ngcd_q_n = gcd(q, n)\r\nsimplified_q_n = q // gcd_q_n, n // gcd_q_n\r\n\r\n# Apply the operation\r\nresult = m * p * (simplified_q_n[0] / simplified_q_n[1])\r\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49.0\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the sum of the series \\(1 + 12 + 123 + 1234 + 12345 + 123456 + 1234567 + 12345678\\) and then find the remainder when this sum is divided by 9. We can use the property of modular arithmetic that allows us to take the modulo at each step of the calculation to avoid dealing with extremely large numbers.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the sum of the series. Then we will take the sum modulo 9 to find the value of \\(n\\).\n\nHere is the Python code to perform these calculations:\n```python\n# Define the series\nseries = [1, 12, 123, 1234, 12345, 123456, 1234567, 12345678]\n\n# Calculate the sum of the series\ntotal_sum = sum(series)\n\n# Find the remainder when the sum is divided by 9\nn = total_sum % 9\nprint(n)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe value of \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "