\((x-2)(x-3)\\), the remainder is \\(2R(x) = 2mx + 2n\\). So we can write:\n\\[ P(x) = (x-2)(x-3)Q'(x) + 2mx + 2n \\]\nfor some polynomial \\(Q'(x)\\). By substituting \\(x = 2\\) and \\(x = 3\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ P(2) = 2m(2) + 2n = 4m + 2n \\]\n\\[ P(3) = 2m(3) + 2n = 6m + 2n \\]\n4. We now have the following system of equations from the values of \\(P(x)\\) at \\(x = 1, 2, 3, 4\\):\n\\[ P(1) = 1 + a + b + 5 = m + n \\]\n\\[ P(2) = 8 + 4a + 2b + 5 = 4m + 2n \\]\n\\[ P(3) = 27 + 9a + 3b + 5 = 6m + 2n \\]\n\\[ P(4) = 64 + 16a + 4b + 5 = 4m + n \\]\n5. We can solve these equations step by step. First, let's solve for \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) using the equations from \\(P(1)\\) and \\(P(4)\\):\n\\[ 6 + a + b = m + n \\]\n\\[ 69 + 16a + 4b = 4m + n \\]\nNext, let's solve for \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) using the equations from \\(P(2)\\) and \\(P(3)\\):\n\\[ 13 + 4a + 2b = 4m + 2n \\]\n\\[ 32 + 9a + 3b = 6m + 2n \\]\nWe can use these equations to find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Let's start by solving the first pair of equations:\n\\[ m + n = 6 + a + b \\]\n\\[ 4m + n = 69 + 16a + 4b \\]\nSubtract the first equation from the second:\n\\[ 3m = 63 + 15a + 3b \\]\n\\[ m = 21 + 5a + b \\]\nSubstitute \\(m\\) into the first equation:\n\\[ 21 + 5a + b + n = 6 + a + b \\]\n\\[ n = -15 - 4a \\]\nNow let's use the second pair of equations:\n\\[ 4m + 2n = 13 + 4a + 2b \\]\n\\[ 6m + 2n = 32 + 9a + 3b \\]\nSubtract the first equation from the second:\n\\[ 2m = 19 + 5a + b \\]\n\\[ m = \\frac{19 + 5a + b}{2} \\]\nSince we already have \\(m = 21 + 5a + b\\), we can set these equal:\n\\[ 21 + 5a + b = \\frac{19 + 5a + b}{2} \\]\nMultiply through by 2:\n\\[ 42 + 10a + 2b = 19 + 5a + b \\]\n\\[ 23 + 5a + b = 0 \\]\n\\[ b = -23 - 5a \\]\nNow substitute \\(b\\) into the equation for \\(n\\):\n\\[ n = -15 - 4a \\]\nWe can now substitute \\(b\\) and \\(n\\) into the equations for \\(m\\) and \\(n\\):\n\\[ m = 21 + 5a + (-23 - 5a) = -2 \\]\n\\[ n = -15 - 4a \\]\nNow we can find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\