ollows: \\( F_1 = 1 \\), \\( F_2 = 1 \\), and for \\( n \\geq 3 \\), \\( F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2} \\). To find the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can observe the sequence modulo 4. Let's compute the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nF_1 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_3 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_4 &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_5 &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_6 &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_7 &\\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_8 &\\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_9 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{10} &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{11} &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{12} &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms. Therefore, \\( F_{100} \\equiv F_{100 \\mod 6} \\pmod{4} \\). Since \\( 100 \\mod 6 = 4 \\), we have \\( F_{100} \\equiv F_4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\).\n\nSo, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\( \\boxed{3} \\).", 'To find the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can use the property of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4. We can observe the sequence modulo 4 and see if it repeats.\n\nThe Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 starts as follows:\n\\[1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0, \\ldots\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms. Therefore, the 100th term modulo 4 is the same as the 4th term modulo 4 (since \\(100 \\mod 6 = 4\\)).\n\nThe 4th term of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 is 3. So, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is 3.\n\nHere is the Python code to confirm this:\n\n```python\n# Function to find the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return b\n\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).', "The Fibonacci sequence is defined as follows:\n\\[ F_1 = 1, \\quad F_2 = 1, \\quad F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2} \\text{ for } n > 2. \\]\nTo find the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can observe the sequence modulo 4. Let's compute the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4:\n\\[ \\begin{align*}\nF_1 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_3 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_4 &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_5 &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_6 &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_7 &\\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_8 &\\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_9 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{10} &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{11} &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{12} &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 4 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}.\n\\end{align*} \\]\nWe can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms. Therefore, we can find the position of the 100th term in the repeating cycle by computing $100 \\mod 6$:\n\\[ 100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4. \\]\nSo, the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 is the same as the 4th term, which is 3. Thus, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]\n```python\n# We can use a simple Python program to confirm this result by generating the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 and checking the 100th term.\ndef fibonacci_modulo(n, mod):\n    a, b = 1, 1\n    for _ in range(n - 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % mod\n    return a\n\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_modulo(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```", "The Fibonacci sequence is defined as follows:\n\\[ F_1 = 1, \\]\n\\[ F_2 = 1, \\]\n\\[ F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2} \\text{ for } n > 2. \\]\n\nWe are asked to find the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4. To do this, we can compute the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 and look for a pattern.\n\nLet's compute the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4:\n\\[ F_1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_3 \\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_4 \\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_5 \\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_6 \\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_7 \\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_8 \\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_9 \\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{10} \\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{11} \\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{12} \\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{13} \\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{14} \\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{15} \\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{16} \\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{17} \\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{18} \\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{19} \\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{20} \\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{21} \\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{22} \\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{23} \\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{24} \\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{25} \\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{26} \\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{27} \\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{28} \\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{29} \\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\]\n\\[ F_{30} \\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}. \\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms. Therefore, the 100th term modulo 4 will be the same as the 4th term modulo 4, which is 3. So the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to compute the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 1, 1\n    for _ in range(3, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return b\n\n# Compute the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence and then determine the remainder when this term is divided by 4. However, calculating the 100th term directly can be computationally intensive. Instead, we can use the property of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 to find the remainder.\n\nThe Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 repeats every 6 terms. This is known as the Pisano period for modulo 4. The sequence modulo 4 is: 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0, ... So, to find the 100th term modulo 4, we need to find the position of 100 within the repeating cycle.\n\nSince the sequence repeats every 6 terms, we can find the remainder when 100 is divided by 6, which is 4. This means the 100th term modulo 4 is the same as the 4th term modulo 4, which is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return b\n\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nfib_100_mod_4 = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(fib_100_mod_4)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence and then determine the remainder when this term is divided by 4. Instead of calculating the 100th term directly (which would be computationally expensive), we can use the property of modular arithmetic to simplify the problem.\n\nThe Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 repeats every 6 terms. This is known as the Pisano period for modulo 4. The first 6 terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 are:\n\\[1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0\\]\nThis pattern repeats indefinitely. Therefore, the 100th term modulo 4 is the same as the 4th term modulo 4, since \\(100 \\mod 6 = 4\\).\n\nThe 4th term in the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 is 3. So, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\r\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\r\n    if n == 0:\r\n        return 0\r\n    elif n == 1:\r\n        return 1\r\n    a, b = 0, 1\r\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\r\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\r\n    return b\r\n\r\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\r\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\r\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", 'To solve the problem of finding the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can use the property of the sequence known as "Pisano periods." The sequence of remainders when Fibonacci numbers are divided by a fixed integer (in this case, 4) eventually becomes periodic. This period is known as the Pisano period.\n\nLet\'s first generate the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 and find its Pisano period. We can then use this period to find the remainder of the 100th term.\n\nHere is the step-by-step code to solve this problem:\n\n1. Generate the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 until we detect a repeating pattern.\n2. Identify the length of the repeating pattern (Pisano period).\n3. Use the Pisano period to find the remainder of the 100th term.\n\nLet\'s implement this in Python:\n```python\ndef find_pisano_period(modulus):\n    # Initialize the first two terms of the Fibonacci sequence\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    # List to store the sequence of remainders\n    sequence = []\n    \n    # Generate the Fibonacci sequence modulo the given modulus\n    while True:\n        # Append the current term to the sequence\n        sequence.append(a)\n        # Calculate the next term\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % modulus\n        # Check if we have seen this pair of terms before\n        if (a, b) == (0, 1):\n            # If we have, we have found the Pisano period\n            return len(sequence)\n        # If the sequence length exceeds the modulus, it\'s safe to assume the period is not yet complete\n        if len(sequence) > modulus * modulus:\n            return len(sequence)\n\n# Find the Pisano period for modulus 4\npisano_period = find_pisano_period(4)\n\n# The 100th term in the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4\nremainder_100th_term = sequence[(100 - 1) % pisano_period]\n\nprint(remainder_100th_term)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name \'sequence\' is not defined\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the variable scope. Let\'s fix this by ensuring the sequence is properly defined and accessible within the function. I\'ll also add some print statements to help debug the process.\n\nHere\'s the corrected code:\n```python\ndef find_pisano_period(modulus):\n    # Initialize the first two terms of the Fibonacci sequence\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    # List to store the sequence of remainders\n    sequence = []\n    \n    # Generate the Fibonacci sequence modulo the given modulus\n    while True:\n        # Append the current term to the sequence\n        sequence.append(a)\n        # Calculate the next term\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % modulus\n        # Check if we have seen this pair of terms before\n        if (a, b) == (0, 1):\n            # If we have, we have found the Pisano period\n            return len(sequence)\n        # If the sequence length exceeds the modulus, it\'s safe to assume the period is not yet complete\n        if len(sequence) > modulus * modulus:\n            return len(sequence)\n\n# Find the Pisano period for modulus 4\npisano_period = find_pisano_period(4)\n\n# Generate the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 up to the Pisano period\nfib_mod_4 = []\na, b = 0, 1\nfor _ in range(pisano_period):\n    fib_mod_4.append(a)\n    a, b = b, (a + b) % 4\n\n# The 100th term in the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4\nremainder_100th_term = fib_mod_4[(100 - 1) % pisano_period]\n\nprint(remainder_100th_term)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).', "To solve this problem, we need to find the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence and then find its remainder when divided by 4. However, instead of computing the 100th term directly, we can observe the pattern in the sequence modulo 4. This approach will simplify our calculations.\n\nLet's first generate the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 and observe the pattern:\n\n1. The first two terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 are 1 and 1.\n2. The next term is the sum of the previous two terms modulo 4.\n\nWe can generate the sequence and observe if it repeats:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nF_1 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_3 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_4 &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_5 &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_6 &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_7 &\\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_8 &\\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms: 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0. Therefore, the 100th term modulo 4 will be the same as the 4th term modulo 4 (since 100 mod 6 = 4).\n\nSo, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is the same as the remainder when the 4th term is divided by 4, which is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code:\n```python\n# Function to generate Fibonacci sequence modulo m and find the nth term\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(n):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return a\n\n# Find the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "The Fibonacci sequence is defined by the recurrence relation \\( F_n = F_{n-1} + F_{n-2} \\) with initial conditions \\( F_1 = 1 \\) and \\( F_2 = 1 \\). To find the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can observe the sequence modulo 4. This means we will compute the Fibonacci sequence and take each term modulo 4.\n\nLet's start by computing the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nF_1 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_2 &\\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_3 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_4 &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_5 &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_6 &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_7 &\\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_8 &\\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_9 &\\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{10} &\\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{11} &\\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\nF_{12} &\\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms. Therefore, \\( F_{100} \\equiv F_{100 \\mod 6} \\pmod{4} \\). Since \\( 100 \\mod 6 = 4 \\), we have \\( F_{100} \\equiv F_4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4} \\).\n\nThus, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\( \\boxed{3} \\).\n\nTo verify this, we can write a Python code to compute the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 up to the 100th term.\n```python\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, mod):\n    a, b = 1, 1\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % mod\n    return a\n\n# Compute the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```", "To solve the problem of finding the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can use the property of modular arithmetic. Specifically, we can generate the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 and observe if there is a repeating pattern.\n\nLet's start by generating the first few terms of the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&F_1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_3 \\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_4 \\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_5 \\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_6 \\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_7 \\equiv 1 + 0 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_8 \\equiv 0 + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_9 \\equiv 1 + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_{10} \\equiv 1 + 2 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_{11} \\equiv 2 + 3 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&F_{12} \\equiv 3 + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}, \\\\\n&\\ldots\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the sequence repeats every 6 terms: 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0. Therefore, to find the remainder when the 100th term is divided by 4, we need to find the position of the 100th term in the repeating cycle. Since the sequence repeats every 6 terms, we can find the position by computing $100 \\mod 6$:\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nThis means that the 100th term has the same remainder as the 4th term in the repeating cycle. From the repeating cycle 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 0, we see that the 4th term is 3. Therefore, the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    elif n == 1:\n        return 1\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return b\n\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nremainder = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(remainder)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine the remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4, we can use the property of modular arithmetic. Instead of computing the 100th Fibonacci number directly and then finding the remainder when it is divided by 4, we can compute the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 from the beginning. This will help us identify any repeating patterns.\n\nLet's start by generating the Fibonacci sequence modulo 4 until we see a repeating pattern. We'll then use this pattern to find the 100th term modulo 4.\n\nHere is the Python code to solve this problem:\n```python\n# Function to find the nth Fibonacci number modulo m\ndef fibonacci_mod(n, m):\n    # Initial values for the first two terms of the Fibonacci sequence\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(n):\n        a, b = b, (a + b) % m\n    return a\n\n# Find the 100th Fibonacci number modulo 4\nfib_100_mod_4 = fibonacci_mod(100, 4)\nprint(fib_100_mod_4)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe remainder when the 100th term of the Fibonacci sequence is divided by 4 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To solve \\(513^2 - 487^2\\), we can use the difference of squares formula. The difference of squares formula states that \\(a^2 - b^2 = (a - b)(a + b)\\). In this case, \\(a = 513\\) and \\(b = 487\\). So we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[513^2 - 487^2 = (513 - 487)(513 + 487)\\]\n\nNow we can calculate the values inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[513 - 487 = 26\\]\n\\[513 + 487 = 1000\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[513^2 - 487^2 = 26 \\times 1000 = 26000\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Calculate the value using the difference of squares formula\r\na = 513\r\nb = 487\r\nresult = (a - b) * (a + b)\r\nprint(result)\n```\