ntinuous at \\(n = 5\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]\n```python\n# Let's verify the solution using Python\n\n# Define the function f(n) with a parameter a\ndef f(n, a):\n    if n < a:\n        return 4 * n + 3\n    else:\n        return 7 * n - 12\n\n# Check the continuity at n = a\na = 5\nleft_limit = f(a - 0.0001, a)\nright_limit = f(a + 0.0001, a)\nvalue_at_a = f(a, a)\n\n# Verify that the left-hand limit, right-hand limit, and value at a are equal\nprint((left_limit, right_limit, value_at_a))\n```", "The function \\( f(n) \\) is defined as follows:\n\\[ f(n) = \\begin{cases} \n4n + 3 & \\text{if } n < a, \\\\\n7n - 12 & \\text{if } n \\ge a.\n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the function \\( f(n) \\) to be continuous at \\( n = a \\), the left-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) must equal the right-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\), and both must equal \\( f(a) \\). Mathematically, this means:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to a^-}} f(n) = \\lim_{{n \\to a^+}} f(n) = f(a). \\]\n\nThe left-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) is given by the first part of the piecewise function:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to a^-}} f(n) = 4a + 3. \\]\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) is given by the second part of the piecewise function:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to a^+}} f(n) = 7a - 12. \\]\n\nSince the function is continuous at \\( n = a \\), these two limits must be equal:\n\\[ 4a + 3 = 7a - 12. \\]\n\nWe can solve this equation for \\( a \\):\n\\[ 4a + 3 = 7a - 12. \\]\nSubtract \\( 4a \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 3 = 3a - 12. \\]\nAdd 12 to both sides:\n\\[ 15 = 3a. \\]\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\\[ a = 5. \\]\n\nLet's verify this by substituting \\( a = 5 \\) back into the original function to ensure continuity. The left-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches 5 is:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to 5^-}} f(n) = 4(5) + 3 = 20 + 3 = 23. \\]\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches 5 is:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to 5^+}} f(n) = 7(5) - 12 = 35 - 12 = 23. \\]\n\nSince both limits are equal to 23, the function is indeed continuous at \\( n = 5 \\). Therefore, the value of \\( a \\) is \\( \\boxed{5} \\).", "The function \\( f(n) \\) is defined as follows:\n\\[ f(n) = \\begin{cases} \n4n + 3 & \\text{if } n < a \\\\\n7n - 12 & \\text{if } n \\ge a \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nFor the function \\( f(n) \\) to be continuous at \\( n = a \\), the left-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) must be equal to the right-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\), and both must be equal to the value of the function at \\( n = a \\). Mathematically, this means:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to a^-}} f(n) = \\lim_{{n \\to a^+}} f(n) = f(a) \\]\n\nThe left-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) is given by the first part of the piecewise function:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to a^-}} f(n) = 4a + 3 \\]\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches \\( a \\) is given by the second part of the piecewise function:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to a^+}} f(n) = 7a - 12 \\]\n\nSince the function is continuous at \\( n = a \\), these two limits must be equal:\n\\[ 4a + 3 = 7a - 12 \\]\n\nWe can solve this equation for \\( a \\):\n\\[ 4a + 3 = 7a - 12 \\]\n\\[ 3 + 12 = 7a - 4a \\]\n\\[ 15 = 3a \\]\n\\[ a = 5 \\]\n\nSo the value of \\( a \\) that makes the function continuous is \\( a = 5 \\). Let's verify this by substituting \\( a = 5 \\) back into the function and checking the limits.\n\nThe left-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches 5 is:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to 5^-}} f(n) = 4(5) + 3 = 20 + 3 = 23 \\]\n\nThe right-hand limit as \\( n \\) approaches 5 is:\n\\[ \\lim_{{n \\to 5^+}} f(n) = 7(5) - 12 = 35 - 12 = 23 \\]\n\nSince both limits are equal to 23, the function is indeed continuous at \\( n = 5 \\).\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{5} \\]", "To find the value of \\(a\\) such that the function \\(f(n)\\) is continuous, we need to ensure that the left-hand limit as \\(n\\) approaches \\(a\\) from the left is equal to the right-hand limit as \\(n\\) approaches \\(a\\) f