cle: \\(8^1 = 8\\), \\(8^2 = 64\\) (units digit is 4), \\(8^3 = 512\\) (units digit is 2), \\(8^4 = 4096\\) (units digit is 6), \\(8^5 = 32768\\) (units digit is 8), and so on.\n   - The cycle repeats every 4 powers: 8, 4, 2, 6.\n   - To find the units digit of \\(8^6\\), we see where 6 falls in the cycle. Since \\(6 \\div 4 = 1\\) remainder 2, the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\n2. **Finding the units digit of \\(2^{2010}\\)**:\n   - The units digits of powers of 2 repeat in a cycle: \\(2^1 = 2\\), \\(2^2 = 4\\), \\(2^3 = 8\\), \\(2^4 = 16\\) (units digit is 6), \\(2^5 = 32\\) (units digit is 2), and so on.\n   - The cycle repeats every 4 powers: 2, 4, 8, 6.\n   - To find the units digit of \\(2^{2010}\\), we see where 2010 falls in the cycle. Since \\(2010 \\div 4 = 502\\) remainder 2, the units digit of \\(2^{2010}\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(2^2\\), which is 4.\n\nSo, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is 4, and the units digit of \\(2^{2010}\\) is 4.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of a^b\r\ndef units_digit(a, b):\r\n    # Get the units digit of the base\r\n    units_digit_base = a % 10\r\n    # Find the units digit of the power\r\n    if units_digit_base == 0:\r\n        return 0\r\n    elif units_digit_base == 1:\r\n        return 1\r\n    elif units_digit_base == 2:\r\n        cycle = [2, 4, 8, 6]\r\n        return cycle[(b - 1) % 4]\r\n    elif units_digit_base == 3:\r\n        cycle = [3, 9, 7, 1]\r\n        return cycle[(b - 1) % 4]\r\n    elif units_digit_base == 4:\r\n        cycle = [4, 6]\r\n        return cycle[(b - 1) % 2]\r\n    elif units_digit_base == 5:\r\n        return 5\r\n    elif units_digit_base == 6:\r\n        return 6\r\n    elif units_digit_base == 7:\r\n        cycle = [7, 9, 3, 1]\r\n        return cycle[(b - 1) % 4]\r\n    elif units_digit_base == 8:\r\n        cycle = [8, 4, 2, 6]\r\n        return cycle[(b - 1) % 4]\r