 point \\(D\\) on \\(\\overline{AM}\\) such that \\(AD = 10\\) and \\(\\angle BDC = 3 \\angle BAC\\).\n   - We need to find the perimeter of triangle \\(ABC\\).\n\n2. **Setting Up the Geometry:**\n   - Let \\(M\\) be the midpoint of \\(BC\\). Since \\(AM\\) is the altitude, \\(AM \\perp BC\\).\n   - Let \\(\\angle BAC = \\theta\\). Then \\(\\angle BDC = 3\\theta\\).\n   - Since \\(AB = AC\\), \\(\\angle ABC = \\angle ACB = \\frac{180^\\circ - \\theta}{2}\\).\n\n3. **Using Trigonometry:**\n   - Let \\(BM = MC = x\\). Then \\(BC = 2x\\).\n   - In right triangle \\(ABM\\), \\(AB = \\sqrt{AM^2 + BM^2} = \\sqrt{11^2 + x^2} = \\sqrt{121 + x^2}\\).\n   - Since \\(AD = 10\\), \\(DM = 11 - 10 = 1\\).\n   - In triangle \\(BDC\\), we can use the Law of Sines:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{BD}{\\sin(\\angle BCD)} = \\frac{BC}{\\sin(\\angle BDC)} = \\frac{CD}{\\sin(\\angle CBD)}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(\\angle BDC = 3\\theta\\) and \\(\\angle BAC = \\theta\\), we have \\(\\angle BCD = \\angle ACB = \\frac{180^\\circ - \\theta}{2}\\).\n\n4. **Using the Angle Relationship:**\n   - We know that \\(\\angle BDC = 3\\theta\\) and \\(\\angle BAC = \\theta\\). Using the Law of Sines in triangle \\(BDC\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{BD}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{180^\\circ - \\theta}{2}\\right)} = \\frac{2x}{\\sin(3\\theta)} = \\frac{CD}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{180^\\circ - \\theta}{2}\\right)}\n     \\]\n     Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{BD}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)} = \\frac{2x}{\\sin(3\\theta)} = \\frac{CD}{\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right)}\n     \\]\n     This implies \\(BD = CD\\), and since \\(BD + CD = BC = 2x\\), we have \\(BD = CD = x\\).\n\n5. **Finding \\(x\\):**\n   - Using the Law of Sines in triangle \\(ABD\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{BD}{\\sin(\\angle BAD)} = \\frac{AD}{\\sin(\\angle ABD)}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(\\angle BAD = \\theta\\) and \\(\\angle ABD = \\frac{180^\\circ - 3\\theta}{2}\\), we have:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{x}{\\sin(\\theta)} = \\frac{10}{\\