alues for \\(x\\) are integers from 1 to 15. The greatest possible value for \\(x\\) is 15. Therefore, the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle is \\(8 + 8 + 15 = 31\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code to iterate through the possible values of \\(x\\) and calculate the perimeter.\n```python\n# Define the fixed sides of the triangle\nside1 = 8\nside2 = 8\n\n# Initialize the maximum perimeter\nmax_perimeter = 0\nmax_x = 0\n\n# Iterate through possible values of the third side\nfor x in range(1, 16):  # x must be a whole number between 1 and 15\n    # Calculate the perimeter\n    perimeter = side1 + side2 + x\n    # Update the maximum perimeter if the current one is greater\n    if perimeter > max_perimeter:\n        max_perimeter = perimeter\n        max_x = x\n\nprint((max_perimeter, max_x))\n```\n```output\n(31, 15)\n```\nThe greatest possible perimeter of the triangle is \\(\\boxed{31}\\) units, which occurs when the third side is 15 units long.", "To determine the greatest possible perimeter of a triangle with two sides of length 8 units each and a third side of whole number length, we need to consider the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that the sum of the lengths of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the length of the third side. For a triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), the following must hold true:\n\\[ a + b > c \\]\n\\[ a + c > b \\]\n\\[ b + c > a \\]\n\nIn this problem, the two given sides are \\(a = 8\\) and \\(b = 8\\), and the third side \\(c\\) is a whole number. The inequalities we need to satisfy are:\n\\[ 8 + 8 > c \\]\n\\[ 8 + c > 8 \\]\n\\[ 8 + c > 8 \\]\n\nThe second and third inequalities are always satisfied for any positive value of \\(c\\). The first inequality simplifies to:\n\\[ 16 > c \\]\nor\n\\[ c < 16 \\]\n\nSo, the possible values for \\(c\\) are whole numbers from 1 to 15. The greatest possible value for \\(c\\) is 15. The perimeter \\(P\\) of the triangle is the sum of its sides:\n\\[ P = 8 + 8 + c \\]\nWhen \\(c = 15\\), the greatest possible perimeter is:\n\\[ P = 8 + 8 + 15 = 31 \\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Given sides of the triangle\nside1 = 8\nside2 = 8\n\n# Possible values for the third side (whole number length)\npossible_values = range(1, 16)\n\n# Calculate the maximum perimeter\nmax_perimeter = max(side1 + side2 + c for c in possible_values)\n\nprint(max_perimeter)\n```\n```output\n31\n```\nThe greatest possible perimeter of the triangle, with two sides of length 8 units each and a third side of whole number length, is \\(\\boxed{31}\\).", "To determine the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle, we need to find the maximum possible length for the third side of the triangle while adhering to the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that the sum of the lengths of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the length of the third side.\n\nGiven two sides of the triangle are each 8 units long, let the third side be \\(x\\). According to the triangle inequality theorem:\n1. \\(8 + 8 > x\\) ⟹ \\(16 > x\\) ⟹ \\(x < 16\\)\n2. \\(8 + x > 8\\) ⟹ \\(x > 0\\)\n3. \\(8 + x > 8\\) ⟹ \\(x > 0\\)\n\nCombining these inequalities, we get \\(0 < x < 16\\). Since \\(x\\) must be a whole number, the possible values for \\(x\\) are from 1 to 15. The greatest possible value for \\(x\\) is 15. Therefore, the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle is \\(8 + 8 + 15 = 31\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given sides of the triangle\nside1 = 8\nside2 = 8\n\n# The third side must be a whole number and satisfy the triangle inequality theorem\nmax_third_side = 0\nfor third_side in range(1, 16):  # third side can be from 1 to 15\n    if (side1 + side2 > third_side) and (side1 + third_side > side2) and (side2 + third_side > side1):\n        max_third_side = third_side\n\n# The greatest possible 