atrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nLet's denote the reflection matrix by $R$. The reflection matrix $R$ has the property that $R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Since $R$ is a reflection matrix, it is an orthogonal matrix with determinant $-1$. The general form of a reflection matrix in 2D is given by:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nGiven that $R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$, we can set up the following equations:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5\\cos(2\\theta) \\\\ 5\\sin(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nThis gives us the equations:\n\\[ 5\\cos(2\\theta) = 4 \\]\n\\[ 5\\sin(2\\theta) = 3 \\]\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\nSo the reflection matrix $R$ is:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nNow we can use this matrix to find the image of $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nSo the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ is reflected to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix R\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under the given reflection matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which confirms our earlier calculation.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that maps \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) under this reflection.\n\nA reflection matrix \\(R\\) in 2D can be written as:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nGiven that \\(R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use this information to find \\(\\cos(2\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(2\\theta)\\).\n\nLet's solve for \\(\\cos(2\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(2\\theta)\\):\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nThis gives us:\n\\[ 5 \\cos(2\\theta) = 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 \\sin(2\\theta) = 3 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nNow we can write the reflection matrix \\(R\\):\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the image of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) under this reflection:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nSo, the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is mapped to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\) under the reflection.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) under the given reflection is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\), which confirms our earlier calculation.\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that transforms the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nA reflection matrix $R$ in 2D can be written as:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the normal vector of the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nGiven that the reflection matrix $R$ transforms $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$, we can set up the following equation:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} 5\\cos(2\\theta) \\\\ 5\\sin(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nFrom this, we get two equations:\n\\[ 5\\cos(2\\theta) = 4 \\]\n\\[ 5\\sin(2\\theta) = 3 \\]\nSolving these equations, we find:\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\nNow we can use these values to construct the reflection matrix:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nNext, we need to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection. We do this by multiplying the reflection matrix $R$ with the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nPerforming the matrix multiplication, we get:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nSo the reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which confirms our earlier calculation.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that transforms the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nLet's break down the steps:\n\n1. **Determine the reflection matrix:**\n   A reflection matrix $R$ in 2D can be written as:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   where $a^2 + b^2 = 1$. We need to find $a$ and $b$ such that:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5a \\\\ 5b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   So, we have $5a = 4$ and $5b = 3$. Solving these equations, we get:\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{4}{5}, \\quad b = \\frac{3}{5}\n   \\]\n   Since $a^2 + b^2 = 1$, we can verify that $\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^2 = 1$. Therefore, the reflection matrix is:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection:**\n   We need to compute:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\cdot 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-8 + 9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-6 - 12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\nSo, the reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflected vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which is equivalent to $\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that transforms the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nLet's break down the problem into manageable steps:\n\n1. **Determine the Reflection Matrix:**\n   A reflection matrix $R$ in 2D can be represented as:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   We know that $R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. This gives us the equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5a \\\\ 5b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   From this, we get $5a = 4$ and $5b = 3$. Solving these equations, we get $a = \\frac{4}{5}$ and $b = \\frac{3}{5}$.\n\n2. **Form the Reflection Matrix:**\n   The reflection matrix is:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Apply the Reflection Matrix to the Vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:**\n   We need to compute:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\cdot 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-8 + 9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-6 - 12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to verify our solution.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\na = sp.Rational(4, 5)\nb = sp.Rational(3, 5)\nR = sp.Matrix([[a, b], [b, -a]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = sp.Matrix([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R * v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\nMatrix([[1/5], [-18/5]])\n```\nThe reflection of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under the given reflection matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have this reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nThe general form of a reflection matrix in 2D is given by:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nWe can determine $\\theta$ by noting that the reflection of $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. The vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ has a length of 5 and makes an angle $\\alpha$ with the x-axis, where $\\cos(\\alpha) = \\frac{4}{5}$ and $\\sin(\\alpha) = \\frac{3}{5}$. Since the reflection matrix $R$ reflects the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$, we have:\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nNow, we can use these values to construct the reflection matrix $R$:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nNext, we apply this reflection matrix to the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-8 + 9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-6 - 12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\\[ = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-18}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nThus, the reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix R\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Apply the reflection matrix to the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection matrix takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which is equivalent to $\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that transforms the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nThe general form of a reflection matrix in 2D is given by:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nGiven that the reflection takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$, we can use this information to find $\\theta$. The vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ can be written as $5 \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, so the reflection matrix $R$ should satisfy:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = 5 \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nThis means that the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$ is the reflection of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ over the line of reflection. The angle $\\theta$ can be determined from the coordinates $\\left(\\frac{4}{5}, \\frac{3}{5}\\right)$.\n\nThe reflection matrix can be derived as follows:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere $\\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5} - 1 = -\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5}$. Therefore, the reflection matrix is:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nNow we can use this reflection matrix to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{1}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\frac{1}{5}(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{3}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nSo the reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\nR = np.array([[-1/5, 3/5],\n              [3/5, 1/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 2.2 -0.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which is equivalent to $\\begin{pmatrix} 2.2 \\\\ -0.6 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", 'The reflection takes $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to