 side:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4} \\frac{1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2 \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\right) \\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\right) = \\frac{255}{512} \\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 2:\n\n\\[ 1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n = \\frac{255}{256} \\]\n\nRearranging terms:\n\n\\[ \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n = 1 - \\frac{255}{256} = \\frac{1}{256} \\]\n\nSince $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^8 = \\frac{1}{256}$, we have:\n\n\\[ n = 8 \\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Given values\na = 1/4\nr = 1/2\nS_n = 255/512\n\n# Function to calculate the sum of the first n terms of a geometric sequence\ndef geometric_sum(a, r, n):\n    return a * (1 - r**n) / (1 - r)\n\n# Iterate to find the value of n\nn = 1\nwhile True:\n    if geometric_sum(a, r, n) == S_n:\n        break\n    n += 1\n\nprint(n)\n```\n```output\n8\n```\nThe value of $n$ is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the number of terms \\(n\\) in the geometric sequence \\(\\left\\{\\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{1}{8}, \\frac{1}{16}, \\dots \\right\\}\\) such that the sum of these \\(n\\) terms is \\(\\frac{255}{512}\\).\n\nThe formula for the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of a geometric series is given by:\n\\[ S_n = a \\frac{1 - r^n}{1 - r} \\]\nwhere \\(a\\) is the first term and \\(r\\) is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given sequence:\n- The first term \\(a = \\frac{1}{4}\\).\n- The common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1/8}{1/4} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nSo the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms is:\n\\[ S_n = \\frac{1}{4} \\frac{1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{4} \\frac{1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2 \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n\\