ng the tens digit and \\(b\\) being the units digit. The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a\\).\n\nFrom the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n1. The sum of the digits is 13. So, \\(a + b = 13\\).\n2. The difference between the original number and the reversed number is 27. So, \\(10a + b - (10b + a) = 27\\), which simplifies to \\(9a - 9b = 27\\), or \\(a - b = 3\\).\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na + b = 13 \\\\\na - b = 3\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system of equations by adding the two equations together to eliminate \\(b\\):\n\\[\n(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\implies 2a = 16 \\implies a = 8.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) back into the first equation:\n\\[\n8 + b = 13 \\implies b = 5.\n\\]\nSo, the original number is \\(10a + b = 10 \\cdot 8 + 5 = 85\\), and the reversed number is \\(10b + a = 10 \\cdot 5 + 8 = 58\\).\n\nThe sum of the original number and the reversed number is \\(85 + 58 = 143\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the equations and solve for a and b\r\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\na, b = symbols('a b')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations\r\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\r\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\r\n\r\n# Solve the system of equations\r\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\r\na_val = solution[a]\r\nb_val = solution[b]\r\n\r\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\r\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\r\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\r\n\r\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\r\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\r\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit. According to the problem, we have two conditions:\n\n1. The sum of the digits is 13: \\(a + b = 13\\).\n2. The difference between the number and the number with its digits reversed is 27: \\(10a + b - (10b + a) = 27\\). This simplifies to \\(9a - 9b = 27\\), or \\(a - b = 3\\).\n\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na + b = 13 \\\\\na - b = 3\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations to eliminate \\(b\\):\n\\[\n(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\implies 2a = 16 \\implies a = 8.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) into the first equation:\n\\[\n8 + b = 13 \\implies b = 5.\n\\]\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\). The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\). The sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(85 + 58 = 143\\).\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the equations\r\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\na, b = symbols('a b')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations\r\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\r\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equations\r\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\r\na_val = solution[a]\r\nb_val = solution[b]\r\n\r\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\r\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\r\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\r\n\r\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\r\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\r\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", 'Let the original number be $10a + b$, where $a$ and $b$ are the tens and units digits, respectively. The reversed number is $10b + a$. The sum of the digits is $a + b = 13$. The difference between the original and reversed numbers is $|10a + b - (10b + a)| = |9a - 9b| = 27$. Simplifying, we get $|a - b| = 3$. Since $a + b = 13$, the only possible values for $a$ and $b$ that satisfy both conditions are $a = 8$ and $b =