he digits\na = 8\nb = 5\n\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\noriginal_number = 10 * a + b\nreversed_number = 10 * b + a\n\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "Let's denote the two-digit number as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number. The problem gives us two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the digits is 13. So, we have the equation:\n\\[a + b = 13\\]\n\n2. The difference between the original number and the number with its digits reversed is 27. The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a\\). So, we have the equation:\n\\[10a + b - (10b + a) = 27\\]\n\\[10a + b - 10b - a = 27\\]\n\\[9a - 9b = 27\\]\n\\[a - b = 3\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\(a + b = 13\\)\n2. \\(a - b = 3\\)\n\nWe can solve this system of equations to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Adding the two equations together, we get:\n\\[ (a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\]\n\\[ 2a = 16 \\]\n\\[ a = 8 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) back into the first equation, we get:\n\\[ 8 + b = 13 \\]\n\\[ b = 5 \\]\n\nSo, the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\). The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\). The sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is:\n\\[ 85 + 58 = 143 \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Define the equations and solve for a and b\r\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\na, b = symbols('a b')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations\r\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\r\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\r\n\r\n# Solve the system of equations\r\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\r\na_val = solution[a]\r\nb_val = solution[b]\r\n\r\n# Calculate the original number and the reversed number\r\noriginal_number = 10 * a_val + b_val\r\nreversed_number = 10 * b_val + a_val\r\n\r\n# Calculate the sum of the original number and the reversed number\r\nsum_of_numbers = original_number + reversed_number\r\nprint(sum_of_numbers)\n```\n```output\n143\n```\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can set up a system of equations based on the given information. Let's denote the original two-digit number by \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the digits of the number. Here, \\(a\\) is the tens digit and \\(b\\) is the units digit.\n\n1. The sum of the digits of the number is 13:\n   \\[\n   a + b = 13\n   \\]\n\n2. The difference between the number and the number with its digits reversed is 27:\n   \\[\n   (10a + b) - (10b + a) = 27\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the left side, we get:\n   \\[\n   9a - 9b = 27\n   \\]\n   Dividing both sides by 9, we get:\n   \\[\n   a - b = 3\n   \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na + b = 13 \\\\\na - b = 3\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system of equations by adding the two equations to eliminate \\(b\\):\n\\[\n(a + b) + (a - b) = 13 + 3 \\implies 2a = 16 \\implies a = 8\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a = 8\\) into the first equation:\n\\[\n8 + b = 13 \\implies b = 5\n\\]\n\nSo the original number is \\(10a + b = 10(8) + 5 = 85\\). The number with its digits reversed is \\(10b + a = 10(5) + 8 = 58\\).\n\nThe sum of the original number and the number with its digits reversed is:\n\\[\n85 + 58 = 143\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the equations\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\na, b = symbols('a b')\n\n# Define the equations based on the problem statement\neq1 = Eq(a + b, 13)\neq2 = Eq(a - b, 3)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (a, b))\na_val = solution[a]\nb_val = so