pmatrix}\n\\]\nNow, we need to find the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under this transformation. Any point \\((x, y)\\) on the line can be written as \\((x, 2x + 1)\\). The image of this point under \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nSo, the image of the point \\((x, 2x + 1)\\) is \\((2x - 1, 4x + 1)\\). To find the equation of the line in the form \\(y = mx + b\\), we express \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\). Let \\(X = 2x - 1\\) and \\(Y = 4x + 1\\). We need to express \\(Y\\) in terms of \\(X\\). From \\(X = 2x - 1\\), we can solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[\nX = 2x - 1 \\implies 2x = X + 1 \\implies x = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(x = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\\) into \\(Y = 4x + 1\\):\n\\[\nY = 4 \\left( \\frac{X + 1}{2} \\right) + 1 = 2(X + 1) + 1 = 2X + 2 + 1 = 2X + 3\n\\]\nSo, the equation of the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under the transformation \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\ny = 2x + 3\n\\]\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx, y = sp.symbols('x y')\n\n# Define the transformation matrix M\nM = sp.Matrix([[4, -1], [2, 1]])\n\n# Define the line equation y = 2x + 1\nline_eq = 2*x + 1\n\n# Define the point on the line\npoint = sp.Matrix([x, line_eq])\n\n# Apply the transformation matrix to the point\ntransformed_point = M * point\n\n# Extract the transformed coordinates\nX = transformed_point[0]\nY = transformed_point[1]\n\n# Solve for y in terms of x\nx_expr = sp.solve(X - (2*x - 1), x)[0]\ny_expr = Y.subs(x, x_expr)\n\n# Simplify the expression\ny_expr_simplified = sp.simplify(y_expr)\nprint(y_expr_simplified)\n```\n```output\nIndexError: list index out of range\n```\nIt seems there was an error in the code. Let's correct it and simplify the process. We'll directly com