right) + \\left(\\frac{1}{6} - x\\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n   This equation is satisfied for any $x$ in the range $0 < x < \\frac{1}{6}$. Now, we need to find the probability of obtaining a sum of 7 when two such dice are rolled. The pairs that sum to 7 are $(1,6)$, $(2,5)$, $(3,4)$, $(4,3)$, $(5,2)$, and $(6,1)$. The probability of each pair can be calculated as follows:\n   \\[\n   P(1,6) = \\left(\\frac{1}{6} + x\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6} - x\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(6,1) = \\left(\\frac{1}{6} - x\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6} + x\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(2,5) = \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) = \\frac{1}{36}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(5,2) = \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) = \\frac{1}{36}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(3,4) = \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) = \\frac{1}{36}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(4,3) = \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) = \\frac{1}{36}\n   \\]\n   Adding these probabilities together, we get:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{sum of 7}) = 2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6} + x\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6} - x\\right) + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{36}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{sum of 7}) = 2 \\left(\\frac{1}{36} - x^2\\right) + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{2}{36} - 2x^2 + \\frac{4}{36} = \\frac{6}{36} - 2x^2 = \\frac{1}{6} - 2x^2\n   \\]\n   We know that this probability is equal to $\\frac{47}{288}$, so we can set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{6} - 2x^2 = \\frac{47}{288}\n   \\]\n   Solving for $x^2$, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{6} - \\frac{47}{288} = 2x^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{48}{288} - \\frac{47}{288} = 2x^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{288} = 2x^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x^2 = \\frac{1}{576}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{1}{24}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the probability of obtaining face $F$ is:\n   \\[\n   p_F = \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{24} = \\frac{4}{24} + \\frac{1}{24} = \\frac{5}{24}\n   \\]\n   The probabili