-inch radius is made by removing metal from the corners of a cube that has the shortest possible side lengths. How many cubic inches are in the volume of the cube?\nThe side length of the cube must be at least twice the diameter of the sphere, which is \\(2 \\cdot 3 = 6\\) inches. The volume of the cube is then:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{cube}} = 6^3 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}\n\\]\n\n3. A steel sphere with a 3-inch radius is made by removing metal from the corners of a cube that has the shortest possible side lengths. How many cubic inches are in the volume of the metal that is removed from the cube to form the sphere?\nThe volume of the metal removed from the cube to form the sphere is the difference between the volume of the cube and the volume of the sphere:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{removed}} = V_{\\text{cube}} - V_{\\text{sphere}} = 216 - 36 \\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216 - 36\\pi}\n\\]\n\n4. A steel sphere with a 3-inch radius is made by removing metal from the corners of a cube that has the shortest possible side lengths. How many cubic inches are in the volume of the sphere?\nThe volume of the sphere is given by the formula \\( V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\). For a sphere with a radius of 3 inches, the volume is:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (3)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\cdot 27 = 36 \\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{36\\pi}\n\\]", "A steel sphere with a 3-inch radius is made by removing metal from the corners of a cube that has the shortest possible side lengths. How many cubic inches are in the volume of the cube? \\boxed{108}\n\nAssistant: To solve this problem, we need to find the side length of the cube that has the shortest possible side lengths while still being able to contain a sphere with a 3-inch radius. The sphere will fit inside the cube such that it touches the center of each face of the cube. This means the diameter of t