oots \\(\\{-1, -3, 11\\}\\):\n\\[ a_{n-1} = 2((-1) \\cdot (-3) + (-1) \\cdot 11 + (-3) \\cdot 11) = 2(3 - 11 - 33) = 2(-41) = -82 \\]\nIn both cases, the value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is 82, which is larger than 50.\n\nTherefore, the least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is 50. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the roots\nroots1 = [1, -3, 11]\nroots2 = [-1, 3, -11]\nroots3 = [1, 3, -11]\nroots4 = [-1, -3, 11]\n\n# Function to calculate a_{n-1}\ndef calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots):\n    n = len(roots)\n    a_n_minus_1 = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        product = 1\n        for j in range(n):\n            if i != j:\n                product *= roots[j]\n        a_n_minus_1 += product\n    return 2 * a_n_minus_1\n\n# Calculate a_{n-1} for each set of roots\na_n_minus_1_1 = calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots1)\na_n_minus_1_2 = calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots2)\na_n_minus_1_3 = calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots3)\na_n_minus_1_4 = calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots4)\n\n# Find the minimum absolute value of a_{n-1}\nmin_abs_a_n_minus_1 = min(abs(a_n_minus_1_1), abs(a_n_minus_1_2), abs(a_n_minus_1_3), abs(a_n_minus_1_4))\nprint(min_abs_a_n_minus_1)\n```\n```output\n50\n```\nThe least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is \\(\\boxed{50}\\).", 'The polynomial $f(x)$ is given by\n$$f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66.$$Since the roots of $f(x)$ are distinct integers, we can write\n$$f(x) = 2(x-r_1)(x-r_2)\\cdots(x-r_n),$$where $r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n$ are the distinct integer roots of $f(x)$.\n\nExpanding the product, we get\n$$f(x) = 2x^n - 2(r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n)x^{n-1} + \\cdots + (-1)^n 2r_1r_2\\cdots r_n.$$\n\nComparing the coefficients of $x^{n-1}$ on both sides, we have\n$$a_{n-1} = -2(r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n).$$\n\nSince the roots are distinct integers, the sum $r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n$ is an integer. Therefore, $a_{n-1}$ is an even integer.\n\nTo minimize $|a_{n-1}|$, we need to minimize the absolute value of the sum of the roots. Since the roots are distinct integers, the smallest possible sum of $n$ distinct integers is $1 + 2 + \\cdots + n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$ is\n$$|a_{n-1}| = 2\\left|\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\right| = n(n+1).$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have $|a_{n-1}| = 2(2+1) = 6$. For $n=3$, we have $|a_{n-1}| = 3(3+1) = 12$. For $n=4$, we have $|a_{n-1}| = 4(4+1) = 20$.\n\nTherefore, the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$ is $\\boxed{6}$.', "To solve this problem, we need to find the polynomial \\(f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66\\) with distinct integer roots. The constant term \\(a_0 = 66\\) is the product of the roots (up to a sign) multiplied by the leading coefficient \\(a_n = 2\\). Therefore, the product of the roots (let's call them \\(r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n\\)) must be \\(\\pm 33\\), because \\(2 \\cdot r_1 \\cdot r_2 \\cdot \\cdots \\cdot r_n = 66\\).\n\nThe possible factorizations of 33 into distinct integers are:\n1. \\(33 = 33 \\cdot 1\\)\n2. \\(33 = 33 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-1)\\)\n3. \\(33 = 11 \\cdot 3\\)\n4. \\(33 = 11 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-1)\\)\n5. \\(33 = 33 \\cdot (-1)\\)\n6. \\(33 = -33 \\cdot 1\\)\n7. \\(33 = -11 \\cdot (-3)\\)\n8. \\(33 = -11 \\cdot (-3) \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-1)\\)\n9. \\(33 = -33 \\cdot (-1)\\)\n10. \\(33 = 11 \\cdot (-3)\\)\n11. \\(33 = 11 \\cdot (-3) \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-1)\\)\n12. \\(33 = -11 \\cdot 3\\)\n13. \\(33 = -11 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-1)\\)\n14. \\(33 = -33 \\cdot (-1)\\)\n\nThe possible sets of roots are \\(\\{33, 1\\}\\), \\(\\{11, 3\\}\\), and their negatives and combinations with \\(-1\\). The polynomial can be written as \\(2(x - r_1)(x - r_2) \\cdots (x - r_n)\\). The coefficient \\(a_{n-1}\\) is the negative of the sum of the roots multiplied by the leading coefficient \\(2\\).\n\nLet's consider the possible sets of roots and calculate the corresponding \\(a_{n-1}\\):\n\n1. For roots \\(\\{33, 1\\}\\), the polynomial is \\(2(x - 33)(x - 1) = 2(x^2 - 34x + 